window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("scoping", {"topics":[{"key":"scoping/customer-discovery","sources":["Rob Fitzpatrick, 2013 — The Mom Test","Steve Blank, 2005 — The Four Steps to the Epiphany"],"why":"A customer engagement usually starts with a solution-shaped request: an executive saw a demo and wants\n\"an agent for procurement\" or \"GPT over our documents\". That request compresses three different things\ninto one sentence: a pain someone feels, a guess about what would relieve it, and a political mandate.\nBuilding the literal request is fast and feels responsive, and it is the most common way an AI project\nends up as a polished demo nobody uses.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. Taking the request at face value means you inherit the\nrequester's guess, which was made without knowing what the technology is good at. Asking users \"would\nyou use a tool that does X?\" produces polite yeses, because hypothetical questions about the future cost\nnothing to answer. Building a quick prototype first and \"seeing what they think\" anchors everyone on\nyour first design and skips the questions that decide success: how often the task happens, what an error\ncosts, who must approve a change, and whether the data is even reachable.\n\nAI raises the stakes on discovery because the system is probabilistic. A conventional feature either\nworks or it does not; an AI feature works on some fraction of cases. Whether 85% is a triumph or a\nliability depends entirely on facts only discovery surfaces: the cost of a wrong answer, whether a human\nreviews the output, and how accurate the current manual process really is.","how":"### 1. Map the people before the problem\nEvery enterprise problem has several roles, and they are rarely the same person:\n- **Economic buyer** — controls the budget and decides whether this continues past a pilot.\n- **Champion** — wants it to succeed and will spend political capital; often the original requester.\n- **End users** — do the work today; their adoption decides whether value is realised.\n- **Data and system owners** — can grant or block access to the sources you need.\n- **Risk functions** — security, legal, compliance, works councils; they have veto power late if\n  ignored early.\n\nYou map these first *because* each sees a different problem. The VP sees headcount cost, the user sees a\ntedious lookup, compliance sees audit exposure. A solution that satisfies only one of them stalls.\n\n### 2. Interview about past behaviour, not future intentions\nThe core technique, popularised by *The Mom Test*, is to ask about specific recent events: \"Walk me\nthrough the last claim you processed. What did you open first? Where did you get stuck?\" Past behaviour\nis evidence; opinions about hypothetical products are not. Useful follow-ups: \"How often does that\nhappen?\", \"What did you do about it?\", \"What have you tried before?\", \"What happens if you get it\nwrong?\". If someone has never tried to solve the problem, it is probably not painful enough to fund.\n\n### 3. Quantify the pain\nTurn anecdotes into a rough annual cost:\n\n```\ncost ≈ volume × (time per item × loaded hourly cost  +  error rate × cost per error)  +  cost of delay\n```\n\nEach term comes from a different person: volume from the system admin or a report, time per item from\nusers, error rate and cost per error from the team lead or QA, cost of delay from the buyer. You need\nthis *because* it bounds the value of any solution and feeds the ROI model; a problem worth 200 hours a\nyear does not justify a six-month integration. Note which term dominates: when errors cost more than\ntime, the design must optimise for accuracy and review, not speed.\n\n### 4. Separate the problem from the proposed solution\nRestate each request as the outcome it serves. \"A chatbot over policy documents\" might really be\n\"adjusters need the applicable coverage clause, with a citation, inside the claims screen\". Asking \"why\"\ntwo or three times usually moves from the solution to the job. Keep the requester's idea as one\nhypothesis, not the specification.\n\n### 5. Collect artifacts, not descriptions\nAsk for real samples: 30 to 50 actual tickets, documents, emails or records, including the ugly ones. A\ndescription of the data is always cleaner than the data. Samples also become the seed of the golden\ndataset and reveal access problems immediately.\n\n### 6. Find the decision process and success evidence\nAsk: \"If this worked, how would you know? Who decides whether it goes to production, and what would they\nneed to see?\" This produces the success criteria and baseline before you build, *because* criteria\ndefined after a demo are negotiated to fit what was built.\n\n### 7. Synthesise and play it back\nWrite a one-page problem statement: who, what job, current cost, constraints, success evidence, open\nrisks, and what you deliberately are not solving. Read it back to the champion and users. Disagreement\nhere is cheap; disagreement after a build is expensive.\n\n### Interview hygiene and when to stop\nInterview users without their manager in the room, *because* people describe the official process in\nfront of the person who owns it. Have a second person take notes so the interviewer can follow up\ninstead of writing. Talk far less than the interviewee and never pitch the solution mid-interview; the\nmoment you describe your idea, answers start rating it rather than describing work. Actively look for\ndisconfirming evidence: ask \"when is this *not* a problem?\" and \"who handles this without trouble?\".\nStop interviewing a role when two or three consecutive interviews add no new pain, workaround or\nexception (saturation), and not before you have covered every role on the map.\n\n### Commitment is the real signal\nEnthusiasm is free. Customers who give you time with real users, sample data, a named sponsor and an\naccess request in flight are committed; those who only say \"this is great\" are not yet.","example":"A worked scenario (all figures illustrative). An insurer's VP of Claims asks for \"a ChatGPT-style\nassistant over our policy wordings\". The FDE runs six 45-minute interviews: the VP, a claims team lead,\nthree adjusters and the claims-system admin.\n\nEach adjuster is asked to walk through their last three claims. A pattern emerges: a typical claim takes\nabout 40 minutes of hands-on work, and roughly 15 of those go to finding which clauses apply, because\nwordings live in a document store organised by product version and adjusters must match the policy's\nissue date to the right version. The team handles about 120 claims a day, so lookup alone is about\n120 × 15 = 1,800 minutes, or 30 person-hours, daily. Errors matter more than time: the team lead says\nwrong-version lookups are a known cause of payment leakage, and supervisors spot-check 10% of claims.\n\nNobody asked for a chat interface; adjusters live in the claims screen and do not want another tab. The\nadmin confirms the policy issue date and product code are available through the claims system's API.\n\nThe played-back problem statement becomes: \"Given an open claim, surface the applicable clauses from the\ncorrect wording version, with citations, inside the claims screen. Success: on 50 historical claims\nlabelled by a senior adjuster, the correct clause appears in the top three for at least the share a\nsenior adjuster agrees is acceptable, and median lookup time drops.\" That is a retrieval-and-citation\nproblem with a hard metadata filter (version by date), not a chatbot, and it now has a baseline, a\nmetric and an integration point.","tradeoffs":["Deeper discovery lowers the risk of building the wrong thing, but delays visible progress; when the sponsor needs momentum, run a narrow prototype in parallel rather than skipping interviews.","Interviewing end users surfaces the real job, but can bypass the champion politically; when the organisation is hierarchical, get the champion to introduce you and share findings with them first.","Quantifying pain precisely makes the ROI case defensible, but costs time; when the problem is obviously large or small, an order-of-magnitude estimate is enough to decide.","Playing back a written problem statement forces alignment, but exposes disagreements early; that discomfort is the point, unless stakeholders are already aligned, when a short confirmation suffices."],"pitfalls":["Asking hypothetical questions like \"would you use this?\"; the symptom is unanimous enthusiasm in interviews followed by near-zero usage in the pilot.","Interviewing only the requester and their managers; the symptom is a solution that fits the org chart but not the screen users actually work in, and users route around it.","Accepting a description of the data instead of samples; the symptom is a prototype that works on clean demo files and collapses on the first real batch of scanned or inconsistent records.","Skipping the decision process; the symptom is a successful pilot that stalls because security, legal or the budget holder were never asked what they needed to see.","Asking leading questions or pitching mid-interview; the symptom is notes full of quotes that agree with the original idea and almost no concrete descriptions of past events, frequencies or workarounds.","Letting the first solution idea become the spec; the symptom is every later conversation being about features of the chatbot rather than whether claims get processed faster or more accurately."],"when":"Run discovery at the start of every engagement and again whenever scope changes materially, such as a\nnew department or a new data source. Keep it proportional: a two-week pilot might need five interviews\nand one sample dataset; a multi-quarter programme needs a stakeholder map and a written problem statement\nsigned off by the buyer. Measure your discovery by whether you can state the job, the current cost, the\ncost of an error, the success evidence and the decision-maker in a page. If you cannot, you are not\nready to build. In interviews for FDE and architect roles, expect a scenario where a customer asks for a\nspecific AI solution; the strong answer reframes it into the underlying problem before proposing anything.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Joins user interviews to see real inputs and edge cases, and turns collected samples into the first evaluation set before writing pipeline code.","architect":"Owns the problem statement and constraints that shape the design, and refuses to commit to an architecture until error cost and integration point are known.","fde":"Runs the interviews on site, maps stakeholders and politics, and converts a solution-shaped executive request into a measurable problem the customer agrees to."},"check":{"q":"A customer's COO asks you to \"build an agent that automates our procurement approvals\" and gives you\ntwo weeks for discovery. What do you do first, and what finding would make you push back on building an\nagent at all?","a":"First map the stakeholders: the COO as buyer, the procurement lead as likely champion, approvers and\nrequesters as users, the ERP owner for data, and finance controls and audit for risk. Then interview\napprovers about their last several approvals: what they checked, where they waited, how often they\nrejected and why. Quantify volume, time per approval and the cost of a wrong approval. I would push back\non an agent if the interviews show most delay is waiting in an approver's queue rather than judgement\nwork, or if approvals are mostly deterministic policy checks (thresholds, approved vendors), because then\nrouting rules or a policy engine fix it more cheaply and auditably, and the AI role shrinks to\nsummarising the exceptions."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which interview question produces the most reliable evidence about a user's pain?","options":["\"Would you use a tool that summarised these documents automatically?\"","\"Walk me through the last time you had to find this information. What did you do?\"","\"On a scale of 1 to 10, how painful is document search for you?\"","\"What features would your ideal AI assistant have?\""],"answer":1,"explanation":"Questions about specific past events reveal actual behaviour, frequency and workarounds. Hypothetical and rating questions invite polite, cheap answers, and feature wish-lists describe solutions, not problems."},{"q":"Why does discovery for an AI system need to establish the cost of a wrong output, more than for a conventional feature?","options":["Because AI systems are more expensive to host than conventional software","Because regulators require error costs for every software project","Because AI output is correct only some of the time, so whether a given accuracy is acceptable depends on error cost and review","Because error cost determines which model provider to choose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A probabilistic system succeeds on a fraction of cases. The same accuracy can be excellent or unacceptable depending on what a mistake costs and whether a human checks, so discovery must surface both."},{"q":"A customer is enthusiastic in every meeting but has not provided sample data or user access after three weeks. What is the best reading?","options":["The project is low-risk because stakeholder sentiment is strongly positive","The team should build with synthetic data so as not to lose momentum","Commitment is not yet real; escalate the access request and the sponsor's role before building","The data is probably clean, so it can be integrated later without risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Enthusiasm costs nothing; giving time, data and access does. Missing samples also hide the data problems that most often sink AI projects, so building on synthetic data defers the real risk."}]},{"key":"scoping/ai-use-case-discovery","sources":[],"why":"Once an organisation decides to \"do AI\", ideas arrive faster than anyone can evaluate them: every team\nhas a list, vendors pitch their own, and the loudest executive's idea tends to win by default. Without a\ndiscipline for choosing, three failure patterns repeat.\n\nFirst, **AI for a deterministic problem**: a task that is really \"apply these five rules\" or \"join these\ntwo tables\" gets an LLM, which is slower, costlier and less reliable than twenty lines of code or a\nreport. Second, **high value, impossible first step**: the most valuable idea (say, autonomous\nunderwriting decisions) is also the one with the highest error cost, the strictest regulation and the\nleast labelled data, so it spends a year in review and never ships. Third, **feasible but worthless**: a\nteam builds a neat summariser for a document nobody reads.\n\nThe naive approach, ranking ideas by value alone or by enthusiasm, fails because value is only one of four\nindependent axes. A use case is a good *first* candidate only when it is valuable, the task genuinely\nneeds pattern-matching over unstructured input, the data and integration point are reachable now, and\nthe consequence of an occasional error is bounded by review or reversibility. Discovery is the process of\nchecking all four before committing engineers.","how":"### 1. Build the candidate inventory from work, not wishes\nCollect candidates by walking real workflows, reading ticket queues and asking teams where time goes\n(see customer discovery and workflow analysis). For each, write one line in the form *\"[role] spends\n[time] doing [task] on [input] to produce [output]\"*. This format forces each idea to name an input and\nan output, *because* a use case without both cannot be built or evaluated.\n\n### 2. Gate on AI fit\nAsk whether the task needs a model at all. Signs it does: inputs are unstructured (free text, documents,\nimages, speech); the mapping from input to output is patterned but too varied for hand-written rules;\nthere is tolerance for imperfect output. Signs it does not: the logic is a stable set of rules, the\nanswer is a database lookup, or every output must be exactly correct with no review. A useful test is\n*verification asymmetry*: tasks where checking an answer is much cheaper than producing it (drafting a\nreply a human approves, extracting fields a human confirms) are strong fits, *because* the human review\nturns a probabilistic component into a reliable system at low cost.\n\n### 3. Estimate value\nUse volume × time per item × loaded cost, plus error and delay costs, and any revenue effect. Keep it\norder-of-magnitude at this stage; the ROI model refines the winners later. Note whether the value needs\nfull automation or only assistance, *because* assisted value (say, half the time saved) is reachable far\nsooner than full automation.\n\n### 3a. Ask what accuracy the value requires\nValue is not independent of accuracy, and the dependence is different for reviewed and unreviewed use\ncases. That difference is what verification asymmetry means in numbers.\n\n*Reviewed (assist).* Let *w* be the minutes to do the item by hand, *e* the minutes to check and edit a\ngood draft, and *r* the minutes wasted reading a bad draft before doing it by hand anyway. With draft\nquality *p*, expected time is `p·e + (1−p)·(r + w)`, so the assistant breaks even at\n`p* = r / (w − e + r)`. With w = 12, e = 3, r = 1 (illustrative), p* ≈ 10%, and at p = 70% expected time\nis 0.7·3 + 0.3·13 = 6 minutes, half the manual time. Mediocre accuracy still pays.\n\n*Unreviewed (automate).* Each error now lands as its full cost *C*, so the break-even error rate is\n`value saved per item ÷ C`. Saving about $8 of handling time per item against a $200 average cost per\nwrong decision allows at most a 4% error rate, so 96% accuracy only breaks even, before any regulatory\nor reputational cost. The same model can therefore be a clear win as an assistant and a loss as an\nautomaton. Both calculations assume reviewers really check; as drafts get better, reviewers start\napproving without reading (automation bias), so measure how many errors the review step actually catches.\n\n### 4. Assess feasibility\nCan you get representative data this month? Is there an integration point where output can land (an\nAPI, a queue, a UI people already use)? Are there examples of correct outputs to evaluate against? Is\nlatency or cost per item compatible with the volume? A candidate blocked on a six-month data-access\napproval is not a first use case regardless of value. For each shortlisted candidate, run a one- or\ntwo-day probe: a plain prompt against 20 to 50 real items, scored by someone who does the work. It will\nnot tell you the final accuracy, but it separates \"plausibly reachable\" from \"the model cannot read\nthese documents at all\" before anyone writes a business case.\n\n### 5. Classify risk\nConsider consequence of error, who is affected (internal staff vs customers vs the public),\nreversibility, regulatory exposure and the sensitivity of the data. This maps onto the organisation's\nAI risk classification; high-risk use cases need governance work that should run in parallel, not be\ndiscovered at launch.\n\n### 6. Rank and pick a portfolio\nPlot surviving candidates on value vs feasibility, with risk as a gate or a penalty. Pick a first use\ncase that is frequent, measurable against a known baseline, human-reviewed, and reachable with current\ndata: it buys trust and builds reusable plumbing (connectors, evaluation harness, access patterns). Keep\none larger strategic bet in discovery alongside it so the programme is not only quick wins.\n\n### 7. Choose the technique last\nOnly after a candidate survives do you decide prompt, RAG, fine-tuning, a workflow or an agent. Choosing\nthe technique first (\"we want an agent\") is how deterministic problems end up with LLMs in them.","example":"A minimal screening pass. Gates remove candidates that do not need a model or cannot start now; the\nrest are scored, with high-risk ones penalised rather than banned so they stay visible as strategic bets.\nAll figures are illustrative inputs a team would estimate in workshops.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Candidate:\n    name: str\n    hours_per_year: float      # volume x time per item, from workflow interviews\n    unstructured_input: bool   # free text, documents, images, speech\n    rule_solvable: bool        # a stable rule set or lookup already answers it\n    data_reachable: bool       # representative samples obtainable this month\n    human_review: bool         # a person checks output before it takes effect\n    error_cost: int            # 1 = trivial, 3 = moderate, 5 = regulatory or customer harm\n\ndef screen(c: Candidate):\n    if c.rule_solvable or not c.unstructured_input:\n        return None, \"automate without a model\"\n    if not c.data_reachable:\n        return None, \"blocked: start the access request now\"\n    risk = c.error_cost - (2 if c.human_review else 0)   # review absorbs some error cost\n    return c.hours_per_year / max(risk, 1), \"candidate\"\n\nideas = [\n    Candidate(\"Draft replies to supplier emails\", 3000, True, False, True, True, 2),\n    Candidate(\"Flag invoices over approval limit\", 1500, False, True, True, False, 3),\n    Candidate(\"Auto-decide loan applications\", 9000, True, False, True, False, 5),\n    Candidate(\"Extract fields from scanned contracts\", 2500, True, False, False, True, 3),\n]\nfor c in ideas:\n    score, verdict = screen(c)\n    print(f\"{c.name:40} {verdict:40} {'' if score is None else round(score)}\")\n```\n\nThe supplier-email drafts score 3,000 (risk 0 floors at 1); the loan decisions, despite three times the\nhours, score 1,800 because they are unreviewed and high-consequence. Invoice limits are a rule, and\ncontract extraction is blocked on data, which tells you what to start in parallel.","tradeoffs":["Starting with a low-risk, reviewed use case builds trust and reusable plumbing quickly, but its value may be modest; when the sponsor needs a headline result to keep funding, pair it with a strategic bet.","A formal scoring matrix makes prioritisation transparent and harder to hijack, but its weights are judgement dressed as numbers; when two candidates score close, decide on qualitative factors instead.","Gating out rule-solvable tasks keeps LLMs where they add value, but some messy rule sets are cheaper to approximate with a model than to codify; that flips when rules change often or are undocumented.","Penalising high-risk use cases speeds the first delivery, but defers the governance learning the organisation will eventually need; run the risk review early when the strategic case depends on it."],"pitfalls":["Choosing the technique before the problem (\"find us agent use cases\"); the symptom is a shortlist where every item is an agent and several are just scheduled scripts with an LLM bolted on.","Ranking by value alone; the symptom is a first project stuck in legal, security or data-access review for months while stakeholders lose patience.","Ignoring where output lands; the symptom is a model that works in a notebook but has no system, screen or queue to deliver into, so nobody uses its results.","Not checking for a measurable baseline; the symptom is a finished pilot where nobody can say whether it improved anything, because the current time or error rate was never recorded.","Assuming human review makes any accuracy acceptable; the symptom is reviewers approving drafts unread once they are usually right, so errors reach customers at the model's raw error rate.","Treating the candidate list as fixed; the symptom is a stale backlog that ignores what the first deployment taught about data quality and user adoption."],"when":"Use this whenever a customer or organisation has more AI ideas than capacity, at the start of an AI\nprogramme, and when choosing the next use case after a first deployment. Run it as a short workshop\nseries: collect candidates from real workflows, screen them together with the people who own the work,\nand leave with two or three shortlisted candidates, each with an input, output, value estimate, baseline\nand risk class. Do not run it when the problem is already fixed by contract; go straight to\ndecomposition. In interviews, expect \"the customer has twenty AI ideas, which do you start with?\" and\nanswer with the four axes, not with the most exciting idea.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sanity-checks feasibility of shortlisted candidates by pulling sample data, trying a quick prompt and estimating per-item latency and cost before anyone commits.","architect":"Owns the portfolio view: which use cases share connectors, evaluation tooling and data access, and therefore which sequence builds a reusable platform.","fde":"Facilitates the use-case workshop with the customer's teams, keeps executive pet projects honest against the screening criteria, and starts access requests for blocked candidates."},"check":{"q":"A bank's innovation team brings you two candidates: AI that writes first drafts of responses to\ncustomer complaints for agents to edit, and AI that decides whether to refund disputed card transactions.\nThe second is worth more. Which do you recommend first, and why?","a":"The complaint drafts. Both involve unstructured text, but the drafts have strong verification\nasymmetry: an agent reads and edits each one, so occasional errors are caught cheaply and the value\n(time saved per complaint) is measurable against current handling time. Refund decisions act directly on\ncustomer money, are likely to be regulated and auditable, need explanation for each decision, and\nprobably lack clean labelled outcomes. They belong in the portfolio as a strategic bet with a parallel\nrisk review, perhaps starting as decision support for a human. Starting there would likely stall in\ngovernance and risk the programme's credibility."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which property most strongly suggests a task is a good first AI use case?","options":["It has the highest estimated annual value in the inventory","Checking an output is much cheaper than producing it, and a person reviews outputs before they take effect","It can be fully automated with no human involvement from day one","It uses the newest model capabilities the vendor has released"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Verification asymmetry plus human review turns a probabilistic component into a reliable system at low cost. Highest value often comes with the highest risk and access hurdles, which stall first projects."},{"q":"A candidate is \"flag purchase orders above the approval limit for the department\". What is the right screening outcome?","options":["A strong AI candidate, because it is high-volume and repetitive","A candidate for fine-tuning, because approval limits are company-specific","Solve it with a rule or a query, because the logic is a stable threshold check on structured data","Block it until the team can label thousands of historical purchase orders"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The task is a deterministic comparison on structured fields. A model would add cost, latency and error to a problem that a rule answers exactly; repetitiveness alone does not make a task need AI."},{"q":"Why choose the implementation technique (RAG, fine-tuning, agent) only after a use case survives screening?","options":["Because techniques change too fast to plan for","Because the vendor contract usually determines the technique","Because choosing the technique first biases the inventory toward problems that fit it, including ones that need no model","Because screening criteria are different for each technique"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Starting from \"we want an agent\" turns discovery into a search for agent-shaped problems and lets deterministic tasks sneak in. The task's input, output, error cost and data should decide the technique."}]},{"key":"scoping/problem-decomposition","sources":[],"why":"A customer goal arrives as one sentence: \"automate invoice exception handling\". Handed to a model as one\nbig prompt, it produces a demo that works on the three invoices you tried and fails opaquely on the\nfourth. When it fails you cannot tell whether the PDF was misread, the wrong field was extracted, the\npurchase order lookup missed, or the model reasoned badly, so every fix is a prompt tweak that might\nbreak something else.\n\nTwo forces make decomposition essential for AI work specifically. First, **errors compound**: if a task\nsilently requires five steps that each succeed 95% of the time independently, the whole chain succeeds\nabout 0.95⁵ ≈ 77% of the time. You only see this, and only know which step to improve, if the steps are\nexplicit. Second, **most of a business process is not a model problem**: lookups, joins, threshold checks\nand API calls are deterministic, and putting them inside a prompt makes exact operations approximate.\n\nThe naive alternative, letting an agent work out the steps at run time, moves the decomposition from\ndesign time, where you can test it, to run time, where it varies per request. Agent planning has its\nplace (see task decomposition in the agents module), but you still need a design-time decomposition to\nknow what the agent is supposed to do, what tools it needs and how to judge whether it did it.","how":"### 1. State the goal as an observable output\nWrite down what exists when the problem is solved: \"each invoice exception is routed to the right owner\nwith a reason code and a drafted resolution, within one business day\". An output you can observe can be\ntested; an aspiration (\"streamline AP\") cannot.\n\n### 2. Work backwards from the output\nAsk what must be true immediately before the output can be produced, and repeat. Working backwards keeps\nevery sub-problem tied to the goal, *because* forward brainstorming produces steps nobody needs.\n\n### 3. Split at natural seams\nGood cut points are where the **data type changes** (scanned image to text, text to structured fields),\nwhere a **decision** is made, where a **system boundary** is crossed (reading the ERP, sending an\nemail), and where **responsibility** passes between people. Seams are where you can put a test and an\ninterface, *because* the artefact passed across them is concrete and inspectable.\n\n### 4. Write a contract for each node\nFor each sub-problem record: input schema, output schema, technique, owner and acceptance test. The\ntechnique choice is the heart of the exercise:\n- **Deterministic code** for lookups, joins, arithmetic, rules and API calls.\n- **Classical ML or a classifier** for high-volume, fixed-label decisions with labelled history.\n- **Retrieval** where the answer exists in a corpus and must be cited.\n- **LLM generation or extraction** for unstructured input and open-ended output.\n- **Human** for high-consequence or low-volume judgements.\nPush work toward the top of this list wherever it suffices, *because* every probabilistic node needs an\nevaluation set and contributes compounding error.\n\nStop splitting a node when it has exactly one technique, one owner and an acceptance test you could\nwrite today. Splitting further adds interfaces without adding testability; a node that still needs two\ntechniques, or whose test you cannot state, is not finished.\n\n### 5. Check coverage and independence\nThe sub-problems should cover the goal with no gaps and little overlap (the MECE test from consulting:\nmutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive). Gaps show up as \"and then somehow\" in the walkthrough;\noverlaps show up as two components that both decide the same thing.\n\n### 6. Estimate per-node quality and find the critical node\nGive each probabilistic node a rough expected accuracy, multiply along the chain, and see which node\ndominates the loss. Add mitigations where they are cheapest: a human review on one node, a deterministic\nvalidator after an extraction (does the amount equal the sum of line items?), or a confidence threshold\nthat routes uncertain cases to a person.\n\nMitigations change *what* you multiply. Separate a node's outcomes into correct, **escalated** (caught\nand sent to a person) and **silent error** (wrong and passed on). If a node is wrong with rate *q* and a\nvalidator catches a fraction *c* of those errors, its silent-error rate falls to `q·(1 − c)` and its\nescalation rate is `q·c` (plus any correct items the check flags by mistake). Silent errors are what\ncompound and reach users; escalations cost reviewer time and lower the automation rate. So report two\nend-to-end numbers, the share of items fully automated and correct, and the share that went wrong\nsilently, *because* a design that trades silent errors for escalations can be a large improvement even\nthough its \"automation rate\" goes down.\n\n### 7. Prototype the riskiest node first\nThe node with the most uncertainty, usually the one with the messiest input, gets a spike before\nanything else is built, *because* if it cannot reach the required quality the whole design changes.\n\n### 8. Keep the tree as a living artefact\nThe decomposition becomes the backbone of the statement of work, the requirements trace and the\nevaluation plan: one test suite per node plus an end-to-end suite.","example":"Invoice exception handling, decomposed into five nodes with rough per-node success rates a team might\nestimate from a spike (illustrative). The script multiplies along the chain, assuming independent\nfailures, and shows which single improvement raises end-to-end success most.\n\n```python\nfrom math import prod\n\nnodes = [  # (name, technique, estimated success rate)\n    (\"parse PDF to text\",                 \"OCR + layout parser\",   0.98),\n    (\"extract vendor, amount, PO number\", \"LLM extraction\",        0.93),\n    (\"match invoice to PO in ERP\",        \"deterministic join\",    0.99),\n    (\"classify exception reason\",         \"LLM classifier\",        0.88),\n    (\"draft resolution email\",            \"LLM generation\",        0.95),\n]\n\ndef end_to_end(rates):\n    return prod(rates)\n\nbase = [r for _, _, r in nodes]\nprint(f\"end-to-end: {end_to_end(base):.1%}\")          # about 75%\n\nfor i, (name, tech, r) in enumerate(nodes):\n    improved = base.copy()\n    improved[i] = 0.99                                 # what if this node were near-perfect?\n    print(f\"{name:36} {tech:20} {r:.0%} -> e2e {end_to_end(improved):.1%}\")\n```\n\nEnd-to-end success is about 75%, worse than any single node. Raising the classifier from 88% to 99% lifts\nthe chain to about 85%, while raising the extractor to 99% reaches only about 80%; the ERP join is already\nat 99%, so the \"improvement\" there changes nothing. That points effort at the classifier, perhaps by adding\na fixed reason-code list, few-shot examples, or routing low-confidence cases to a human. Treat the product\nas a rough guide, not a forecast: real failures are often correlated (a bad scan breaks both parsing and\nextraction), and a validator that catches an error before it propagates breaks the simple multiplication.","tradeoffs":["Fine-grained decomposition makes each node testable and replaceable, but adds interfaces, latency and integration code; when one model call handles two steps reliably, merging them is simpler.","Moving logic into deterministic code gives exactness and auditability, but brittle rules break on variation; it flips when the inputs are too varied to enumerate rules for.","Explicit pipelines are predictable and debuggable, but cannot adapt to cases the designer did not foresee; when the space of paths is genuinely open-ended, an agent with tools fits better.","Per-node evaluation finds the weak link quickly, but node metrics can all look fine while the end-to-end result is poor; always keep an end-to-end test alongside the node tests."],"pitfalls":["Decomposing by org chart or by existing system instead of by data flow; the symptom is nodes whose outputs nobody consumes and handoffs that recreate today's delays.","Putting deterministic steps inside the prompt; the symptom is arithmetic errors, invented IDs or wrong dates in otherwise fluent output.","Treating per-node accuracy as end-to-end accuracy; the symptom is a team reporting 90%+ on every component while users see a large share of cases go wrong.","Leaving node outputs unstructured; the symptom is downstream code parsing free text with regexes and failing whenever the model rephrases.","Reporting a single success rate that mixes escalations and silent errors; the symptom is a stakeholder dispute where operations say \"too many items reach the queue\" and risk says \"too many errors reach customers\", and neither can be traded off because they were never measured separately.","Building the easy nodes first; the symptom is a mostly finished pipeline blocked for weeks on the one hard extraction that should have been spiked on day one."],"when":"Decompose before estimating, before writing a statement of work and before choosing a model. It is\nworth doing even for small builds, because the output (a list of nodes with contracts and tests) is the\nevaluation plan. Skip heavy decomposition only for a genuinely single-step task, such as summarising one\ndocument type for internal reading. Measure success by whether every node has an owner, a technique and\na test, and whether you can name the node that dominates end-to-end error. In interviews, decomposing a\nvague goal aloud, marking which parts are deterministic, and multiplying error rates is a strong signal\nof senior judgement.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements each node behind a typed interface with its own tests, and adds validators between nodes so failures are caught at the seam where they occur.","architect":"Owns the decomposition itself: where the seams go, which nodes are deterministic, where humans sit, and how node contracts map to services and evaluation suites.","fde":"Decomposes the customer's goal in a workshop with their subject experts, and uses the tree to agree scope, ownership and what \"done\" means for each piece."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a system that \"answers any question about our 10,000 contracts\". How would you\ndecompose it, and which node would you prototype first?","a":"Start from observable outputs: an answer with citations to clauses, or \"not found\". Split into:\ndocument ingestion and parsing (scans, tables); metadata extraction (counterparty, dates, contract type)\ninto structured fields; question routing, since \"which contracts expire next quarter?\" is a structured\nquery over extracted fields, not retrieval; clause retrieval for open questions; answer generation\nconstrained to retrieved text; and citation checking. Deterministic parts are the date queries and\nfilters. I would spike parsing and metadata extraction on a sample of the ugliest real contracts first,\nbecause everything else depends on them and scanned legal documents are where quality is most uncertain."},"quiz":[{"q":"A pipeline has four independent probabilistic steps with success rates 0.95, 0.90, 0.97 and 0.92. What is the approximate end-to-end success rate?","options":["About 93%, the average of the four","About 90%, the weakest step","About 76%, the product of the four","About 97%, the strongest step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With independent failures the chain succeeds only when every step succeeds, so rates multiply: 0.95 × 0.90 × 0.97 × 0.92 ≈ 0.76. Averages and the weakest step both overstate end-to-end quality."},{"q":"Which is the best seam at which to split a sub-problem?","options":["Between two teams' sprint boundaries","Where the data changes form, such as scanned image to text or text to structured fields","After every model call, regardless of what it produces","At the point where the prompt exceeds a fixed token budget"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A change of data form produces a concrete, inspectable artefact that can carry a contract and a test. Splits by team or token count do not correspond to anything you can verify."},{"q":"In the decomposition, why move a purchase-order lookup out of the LLM and into code?","options":["Because LLMs cannot read purchase-order numbers","Because code is always cheaper to maintain than prompts","Because the lookup is exact and deterministic, and doing it in a prompt makes an exact operation approximate","Because ERP systems forbid access from LLMs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A join or lookup has one correct answer that code returns every time. Inside a prompt it gains error rate, cost and latency while adding nothing, and becomes a compounding failure point."}]},{"key":"scoping/workflow-analysis","sources":["Mike Rother and John Shook, 1999 — Learning to See","Wil van der Aalst, 2016 — Process Mining: Data Science in Action"],"why":"AI projects are usually pitched at a task: \"summarise the ticket\", \"draft the reply\", \"extract the\nfields\". The customer, however, cares about an outcome of the whole workflow: tickets resolved faster,\nclaims paid correctly, fewer escalations. The link between the two is the workflow, and it is where many\nAI deployments quietly fail to deliver.\n\nTwo facts make the naive approach, automating the most visible task, unreliable. First, in most\nknowledge-work processes the elapsed time is dominated by **waiting**, not working: items sit in queues,\nwait for an approver, or wait for missing information from someone else. If an AI halves the time an\nagent spends researching a ticket but the ticket then waits two days for approval, the customer-visible\ncycle time barely moves. Lean practice calls the ratio of working time to elapsed time *flow efficiency*,\nand in office processes it is commonly a small fraction, so most of the opportunity sits in the waits.\n\nSecond, the **documented process is not the real one**. Process diagrams show the happy path; real work\nincludes exception paths, rework loops, side channels (spreadsheets, chat messages, personal email) and\ntacit checks that experienced staff perform without writing them down. An AI system designed from the\nofficial diagram breaks on the cases that make up much of the actual volume, or skips a check that\nquietly mattered. Workflow analysis exists to see the real flow before deciding where AI belongs.","how":"### 1. Fix the scope and the unit of work\nChoose one workflow and its unit: a ticket, a claim, an invoice, a contract. Define where it starts and\nends (complaint received to complaint closed). Everything is measured per unit, *because* averages across\nmixed units hide the bottleneck.\n\n### 2. Observe, do not only interview\nSit with people doing the work (\"go to the gemba\", in lean terms) and watch several real items end to\nend. Interviews give the process people believe they follow; observation shows the copy-paste between\nsystems, the lookup in a personal spreadsheet and the colleague they message for an answer.\n\n### 3. Map the steps with actors and systems\nDraw a swimlane diagram: one lane per role or system, boxes for steps, arrows for handoffs, diamonds for\ndecisions. For each step capture who does it, which system they use, what input they need and what they\nproduce. Handoffs are marked explicitly *because* every handoff is a queue and a chance for context loss.\n\n### 4. Measure touch time and wait time\nFor each step record *touch time* (someone actively working) and *wait time* (the item sitting before the\nstep starts). This is the core of a value stream map. Where an event log exists (ticket history, case\nmanagement audit trail, ERP change log) mine it: every record of case ID, activity and timestamp lets you\ncompute waits and paths for thousands of cases instead of the handful you observed. This is the basis of\nprocess mining.\n\n### 5. Enumerate variants and exceptions\nCount how many distinct paths items take and how frequent each is. Track rework loops (an item sent back),\nescalations and \"request more information\" steps. Design for the variants that carry the volume, and\ndecide explicitly which rare variants stay manual.\n\n### 6. Locate the constraint\nUsing the measurements, find the step that limits throughput or dominates elapsed time. A queue that\nkeeps growing before one step means items arrive faster than that step completes them: its capacity is\nbelow the arrival rate. Little's law (average items in progress = throughput × average time in system,\nfor a stable system) then explains why that hurts: with throughput capped by the constraint, every extra\nitem queued adds directly to time in system. That is where improvement changes the outcome.\n\nA quick sanity check with illustrative numbers: if 200 tickets are open and the team closes 50 a day,\naverage time in system is 200 ÷ 50 = 4 days. Halving research effort does not raise the 50 a day if\napproval is what caps it, so the 4 days stay. Doubling approval capacity, or removing the items that\ndid not need approval, does raise throughput, up to the capacity of the next-slowest step, and the same WIP clears faster. This is the\ntheory-of-constraints rule in one line: an hour saved at a non-constraint is a mirage; an hour saved at\nthe constraint is an hour for the whole system.\n\n### 7. Identify AI insertion points\nNow ask which steps AI could help at, and how: removing a step, shortening touch time, preventing a\nrework loop (by catching missing information at intake), or eliminating a wait (by pre-computing what the\napprover needs). Mark where humans must stay in the loop and what they will review.\n\nThen check where the constraint moves. An AI step that lets agents finish research twice as fast sends\nitems to approval twice as fast; if approval was close to capacity, it becomes the new constraint and\nits queue grows. Every human review you add is also a new step with its own capacity: 400 items a day\nat 2 minutes of review each is over 13 reviewer-hours daily, which someone must staff.\n\n### 8. Draft the to-be workflow and the baseline\nDraw the proposed flow with the AI component, its human checkpoints and the fallback path when it is\nunsure. Record current cycle time, touch time, error and rework rates as the baseline, *because* without\nthem the pilot cannot show improvement.","example":"A support team wants AI to \"write better research notes\". Exporting three tickets from the ticketing\nsystem's history (illustrative data) and splitting each step into touch and wait time shows where time\nactually goes.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict, Counter\n\n# (case_id, activity, start_hour, end_hour) exported from the ticketing system\nlog = [\n    (\"T1\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T1\", \"triage\", 4.0, 4.2), (\"T1\", \"research\", 22.0, 22.6),\n    (\"T1\", \"approve\", 47.0, 47.1), (\"T1\", \"reply\", 49.0, 49.3),\n    (\"T2\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T2\", \"triage\", 2.0, 2.1), (\"T2\", \"research\", 26.0, 26.4),\n    (\"T2\", \"approve\", 50.0, 50.1), (\"T2\", \"reply\", 51.0, 51.2),\n    (\"T3\", \"receive\", 0.0, 0.1), (\"T3\", \"triage\", 6.0, 6.3), (\"T3\", \"research\", 20.0, 20.8),\n    (\"T3\", \"request info\", 21.0, 21.1), (\"T3\", \"research\", 70.0, 70.5),\n    (\"T3\", \"approve\", 93.0, 93.1), (\"T3\", \"reply\", 95.0, 95.2),\n]\ncases = defaultdict(list)\nfor case, act, start, end in log:\n    cases[case].append((start, end, act))\n\ntouch, wait, variants = defaultdict(float), defaultdict(float), Counter()\nfor steps in cases.values():\n    steps.sort()\n    variants[\" > \".join(a for _, _, a in steps)] += 1\n    prev_end = steps[0][0]\n    for start, end, act in steps:\n        wait[act] += start - prev_end      # time the item sat in a queue before this step\n        touch[act] += end - start          # time someone actually worked on it\n        prev_end = end\n\nt, w = sum(touch.values()), sum(wait.values())\nprint(f\"touch {t:.1f}h, wait {w:.1f}h, flow efficiency {t / (t + w):.1%}\")\nfor act in touch:\n    print(f\"{act:13} touch {touch[act]:4.1f}h   wait before {wait[act]:5.1f}h\")\nprint(len(variants), \"variants; most common:\", variants.most_common(1)[0])\n```\n\nAcross the three tickets, 4.3 hours of work sit inside 191 hours of waiting (flow efficiency about 2%).\nResearch touch time is 2.3 hours, but 104 hours are spent waiting before research, including two days in\nT3 waiting for customer information, and 70 hours waiting for approval. Better research notes would save\nlittle; catching missing information at intake and pre-packaging the approver's evidence target the waits.","tradeoffs":["Direct observation reveals tacit steps and side channels, but covers only a handful of cases; when an event log exists, combine both so observation explains what the log measures.","Process mining from event logs gives statistically meaningful paths and waits, but only sees steps that write to a system; work done in email or chat is invisible unless you capture it separately.","A detailed current-state map makes the constraint unarguable, but takes time customers may resent; when the bottleneck is obvious and agreed, map just that segment in depth.","Targeting the constraint maximises outcome improvement, but the constraint may sit in another team or a policy you cannot change; then pick the best step within your mandate and say so explicitly."],"pitfalls":["Analysing the documented process instead of the real one; the symptom is an AI system that handles the happy path but fails on the exception paths that make up much of real volume.","Measuring only touch time; the symptom is a pilot that cuts handling time per item yet leaves customer-visible cycle time unchanged, and the sponsor sees no improvement.","Ignoring side-channel tools; the symptom is users who keep their old spreadsheet open next to the new system, because a check or lookup it supported was never modelled.","Inserting AI without redesigning the human step around it; the symptom is reviewers who either approve everything unread or redo the work from scratch, erasing the saving.","Speeding up one step without checking downstream capacity; the symptom is a queue that shrinks in front of the improved step and grows in front of the next one, with cycle time unchanged.","Not recording a baseline before changing the flow; the symptom is an argument after launch about whether things improved, settled by anecdote."],"when":"Do workflow analysis whenever an AI component will sit inside an existing business process, which is\nalmost always in enterprise and forward-deployed work, and do it before choosing which step to automate.\nKeep it lean for small scopes: observe five to ten items, sketch a swimlane, and pull timestamps for a\nfew hundred cases if a log exists. Go deeper (full value stream map, process mining) when the sponsor's\ngoal is a cycle-time or throughput outcome. Measure flow efficiency, waits per step, variant frequencies\nand rework rates. In interviews, the signal is recognising that the step the customer named is often not\nthe one that limits the outcome.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pulls event logs from ticketing or case systems, computes per-step waits and variants, and wires the AI step to emit the same events so before and after are comparable.","architect":"Decides where AI components and human checkpoints sit in the to-be workflow, and whether orchestration lives in an existing workflow engine or a new service.","fde":"Shadows the customer's staff, uncovers the side channels and tacit checks, and presents the current-state map that convinces the sponsor where the real bottleneck is."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI copilot to speed up loan officers' document review, which takes about an\nhour per application. End-to-end, applications take about ten business days. How would you decide whether\nthe copilot is the right investment?","a":"I would map the workflow and pull timestamps from the loan origination system for a few hundred\nrecent applications, splitting touch time from waits per step and counting variants. If most of the ten\ndays is waiting, for example for missing documents from applicants or for a credit committee that meets\ntwice a week, a review copilot saves officer time but barely changes time to decision. In that case the\nhigher-leverage AI use is at intake: checking completeness and requesting missing documents immediately,\nor preparing the committee pack. If review itself queues because officers are the capacity constraint,\nthe copilot is right, and I would baseline review time and error rate first."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a process with 30 minutes of touch time and 4 days of elapsed time, AI halves the touch time. What most likely happens to elapsed time?","options":["It halves, because the work is done twice as fast","It barely changes, because elapsed time is dominated by waiting between steps","It doubles, because reviewers must check AI output","It drops to 15 minutes, because the queue disappears"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Saving 15 minutes of work inside four days of queueing leaves elapsed time almost unchanged. Unless the touched step is the capacity constraint causing the queue, the waits are where elapsed time lives."},{"q":"Why is direct observation of users valuable even when a detailed event log exists?","options":["Event logs are usually inaccurate about timestamps","Regulations require in-person observation before automation","Logs only record steps that touch a system, missing side channels, tacit checks and workarounds","Observation produces larger sample sizes than logs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logs are excellent for statistics on recorded steps, but spreadsheets, chat questions and mental checks leave no trace. Observation explains what the log measures and reveals what it cannot see."},{"q":"A queue in front of the approval step grows every week. What does that indicate?","options":["The approval step has too much documentation","Items arriving at approval are of lower quality than before","Approval capacity is below the arrival rate, so it is the constraint limiting throughput","The upstream steps are working too slowly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A queue only grows when work arrives faster than the step completes it, so approval capacity is the constraint. Slow upstream steps would shrink this queue, not grow it; and by Little's law, the growing backlog at a capped throughput translates directly into longer time in system."}]},{"key":"scoping/domain-modeling","sources":["Eric Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design"],"why":"Every organisation has a private dialect. In one insurer \"claim\" means a single loss event; in another it\nmeans each coverage line within it. \"Active customer\" might mean \"has a contract\", \"logged in this\nquarter\" or \"billed this month\", depending on whether you ask sales, product or finance. Experienced\nstaff carry these distinctions in their heads, and the documents they write assume them.\n\nA language model brings the opposite: a broad, average sense of each word learned from public text. Ask\nit to \"count active customers\" or \"extract the claim date\" and it picks a plausible generic meaning. The\noutput is fluent and well formatted, so the error is silent: the number is wrong, or the date is the\nreport date rather than the loss date, and the pipeline passes it on.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail. Letting the model infer meaning from context works only when documents are\nconsistent, and they rarely are across departments. Patching each misunderstanding with a sentence in the\nprompt produces a growing pile of special cases that disagree with the database schema, the retrieval\nfilters and the evaluation labels, because each was written separately. Domain modeling replaces the\npatches with one explicit model, agreed with subject-matter experts (SMEs), that every component reads\nfrom. It also forces the questions that change scope: which entity has the identity, which states exist,\nand which rules the system must never violate.","how":"### 1. Build the glossary first\nList the terms that appear in interviews, documents and screens, and write a one-line definition for\neach, with an example and a counter-example. Domain-driven design calls the result the *ubiquitous\nlanguage*: one vocabulary shared by experts, code and documentation. Start here *because* most domain\nerrors in AI systems are vocabulary errors, and a glossary is cheap to validate with SMEs.\n\n### 2. Identify entities and value objects\nAn **entity** has an identity that persists while its attributes change (a claim, a policy, a customer).\nA **value object** is defined only by its attributes (an address, a money amount with currency). The\ndistinction matters for AI *because* extraction must resolve entities to an existing identifier (which\ncustomer is this email about?), while value objects only need to be parsed correctly.\n\nIdentity resolution deserves its own design, because it is where extraction most often silently fails.\nResolve in order of reliability: an exact key found in the text (a claim number matching the agreed\npattern), then a deterministic lookup on strong attributes (policy number plus loss date), then fuzzy\nmatching on weak ones (a name and a postcode), and finally ask a person. Record which rule matched,\n*because* a record linked by a fuzzy name match should be treated with less trust than one linked by key.\n\n### 3. Map relationships and cardinality\nRecord how entities relate: a policy has many claims, a claim has many coverage lines, a customer can\nhold several policies. Cardinality decides retrieval and reasoning: a question about \"the customer's\ndeductible\" is ambiguous when the customer has three policies, so the system must ask or choose by rule.\n\n### 4. Model lifecycles as state machines\nFor each key entity, list its states and the permitted transitions, along with who or what triggers\neach. This is what lets an AI system reason about \"what can happen next\" and stops an agent from taking\nan action the business forbids (paying a claim that was never approved).\n\n### 5. Write down invariants and business rules\nInvariants always hold (a loss date is never after the report date; the payment total never exceeds the\napproved amount). Rules guide decisions (claims above a threshold need a second approver). Enforce\ninvariants in code as validators, *because* a model can be asked to respect them but cannot be trusted\nto always do so.\n\nSome invariants span several entities: \"the sum of payments across a claim's coverage lines never exceeds\nits approved amount\" cannot be checked by looking at one line. DDD groups such entities into an\n**aggregate** with one root (the claim) through which all changes go, so the root can check the rule on\nevery change. For an agent this defines the unit a tool should act on: expose `add_payment(claim_id, …)`\non the claim, not a raw \"update coverage line\" tool that can break the total.\n\n### 6. Draw bounded contexts\nWhere the same word means different things in different parts of the organisation, do not force one\ndefinition. Model each context separately and define the translation between them. In AI terms, each\ncontext often gets its own schema, prompt glossary and retrieval index.\n\n### 7. Project the model into every artefact\nThe same model becomes: typed schemas for structured output and extraction; enums that constrain\nclassification labels; a glossary section in prompts; metadata fields for retrieval filters; entity and\nrelationship types if a knowledge graph is warranted; and the label set and edge cases for evaluation.\nOne source, many projections, *because* divergence between them is where silent errors come from.\n\n### 8. Validate with instances, not diagrams\nSMEs rarely critique a class diagram well, but they spot errors instantly in concrete examples: \"this\nclaim would never be in that state\". Walk real records through the model.","example":"A slice of an insurer's claims domain, agreed with claims SMEs, expressed as a Pydantic v2 schema. The\nsame class constrains LLM extraction output, documents the glossary in field descriptions, enforces an\ninvariant and guards lifecycle transitions that an agent might attempt.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom enum import Enum\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, model_validator\n\nclass ClaimStatus(str, Enum):           # lifecycle agreed with claims SMEs\n    FNOL = \"first_notice_of_loss\"\n    UNDER_REVIEW = \"under_review\"\n    APPROVED = \"approved\"\n    DENIED = \"denied\"\n    PAID = \"paid\"\n\nALLOWED = {                             # which transitions the business permits\n    ClaimStatus.FNOL: {ClaimStatus.UNDER_REVIEW},\n    ClaimStatus.UNDER_REVIEW: {ClaimStatus.APPROVED, ClaimStatus.DENIED},\n    ClaimStatus.APPROVED: {ClaimStatus.PAID},\n    ClaimStatus.DENIED: set(), ClaimStatus.PAID: set(),\n}\n\nclass Claim(BaseModel):\n    claim_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^CLM-\\d{8}$\")         # identity comes from the claims system\n    policy_number: str = Field(description=\"Policy, not the customer account number\")\n    loss_date: date = Field(description=\"Date the loss happened, not the date it was reported\")\n    reported_date: date\n    status: ClaimStatus\n    reserve_amount: Decimal = Field(ge=0, description=\"Estimated liability, not the amount paid\")\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def loss_before_report(self):\n        if self.loss_date > self.reported_date:           # invariant stated by SMEs\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_date cannot be after reported_date\")\n        return self\n\ndef transition(claim: Claim, new: ClaimStatus) -> Claim:\n    if new not in ALLOWED[claim.status]:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{claim.status.value} -> {new.value} is not a permitted transition\")\n    return claim.model_copy(update={\"status\": new})\n\nc = Claim(claim_id=\"CLM-00412345\", policy_number=\"HX-778\", loss_date=\"2026-03-02\",\n          reported_date=\"2026-03-05\", status=\"under_review\", reserve_amount=4200)\nprint(transition(c, ClaimStatus.APPROVED).status.value)   # approved\ntransition(c, ClaimStatus.PAID)   # raises: under_review -> paid is not a permitted transition\n```\n\nThe field descriptions carry the glossary into the JSON schema the model sees, the enum restricts status\nto the agreed states, and the validator rejects an extraction that swaps the loss and report dates. The\nreserve is a `Decimal` rather than a `float` because money is a value object whose arithmetic must be\nexact; binary floats cannot represent most decimal amounts and accumulate rounding error in totals.","tradeoffs":["An explicit model prevents silent vocabulary errors, but takes SME time to agree; when the task is a narrow, single-team summarisation, a short glossary in the prompt may be all you need.","Strict schemas and enums make outputs reliable and testable, but reject legitimate cases the model did not foresee; add an explicit \"other\" or escalation path when the domain is still being discovered.","Separate bounded contexts keep each definition precise, but require translation code between them; that pays off when departments genuinely disagree, and is overhead when they do not.","A knowledge graph makes relationships queryable and explainable, but is costly to build and keep in sync; it is worth it when questions routinely traverse several relationships, not for flat lookups."],"pitfalls":["Defining terms from documents alone, without SMEs; the symptom is a glossary that reads well but gets corrected on almost every example walked through with the people who do the work.","Letting prompts, schemas and evaluation labels drift apart; the symptom is a model that passes its own tests while users report it uses terms differently from the reporting dashboards.","Forcing one meaning across bounded contexts; the symptom is endless debates over a field definition and outputs that are right for one department and wrong for another.","Enforcing invariants only through prompt instructions; the symptom is a rare but recurring output that violates a rule the business considers impossible, discovered by an auditor.","Giving an agent write tools on individual records inside an aggregate; the symptom is each update passing its own validation while the combination violates a cross-record rule, such as payments exceeding the approved total.","Modelling entities without identity resolution; the symptom is extracted records that name a customer or policy but cannot be linked to the system of record, so downstream steps fail."],"when":"Model the domain whenever the AI system extracts, classifies or acts on business entities, whenever\nseveral teams or systems share data, and whenever outputs feed reports or decisions. Do it right after\ndiscovery and workflow analysis, before writing prompts or schemas, and keep it lightweight: a glossary,\nkey entities, one or two state machines and the invariants. Measure it by how many SME corrections\nremain when you walk real records through it and by whether every schema, enum and evaluation label\ntraces back to it. In interviews, explaining how one domain model projects into prompts, schemas,\nretrieval filters and evaluation labels shows architectural maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Encodes the model as typed schemas, enums and validators used for structured outputs, and rejects or escalates model outputs that violate invariants instead of passing them on.","architect":"Owns bounded-context boundaries and the canonical model, deciding where translation happens and whether a knowledge graph or relational schema represents the domain.","fde":"Runs glossary and example-walkthrough sessions with the customer's experts, and catches the terms that mean different things in different departments before they reach a prompt."},"check":{"q":"A retail customer wants an assistant that answers \"how many active customers do we have in each\nregion?\" from their warehouse and documents. Sales, finance and marketing each define \"active\"\ndifferently. How do you handle it?","a":"Treat it as a bounded-context problem, not a prompt problem. Agree each definition with its owning\nteam, give each a distinct name in the glossary (contracted, billed in the last 30 days, engaged in the\nlast 90 days), and implement each as a deterministic metric in the warehouse or semantic layer, not as\nsomething the model computes. The assistant's job becomes mapping a question to the right named metric\nand asking for clarification when \"active\" is ambiguous, citing which definition it used. Evaluation\ncases then include ambiguous phrasings to confirm it asks or chooses by an agreed default."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should invariants such as \"loss date is never after report date\" be enforced in code rather than only stated in the prompt?","options":["Prompts cannot contain dates","Code validation is required by every regulator","A model can be instructed to respect a rule but cannot be guaranteed to, while a validator rejects every violation","Validators make the model generate faster"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Instructions shift probabilities; they do not guarantee behaviour. A deterministic validator catches every violation, so impossible outputs are rejected or escalated instead of silently passed downstream."},{"q":"What distinguishes an entity from a value object in a domain model?","options":["Entities are stored in databases, value objects only in memory","An entity keeps its identity while attributes change; a value object is defined only by its attributes","Entities are nouns and value objects are verbs","Value objects always come from external systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A claim remains the same claim when its status changes, so it needs an identifier that extraction must resolve. An address or money amount is equal to any other with the same values and only needs parsing."},{"q":"Two departments use \"order\" to mean different things. What is the most robust modelling choice?","options":["Pick the larger department's definition and use it everywhere","Let the model infer which meaning is intended from context","Model separate bounded contexts with distinct definitions and an explicit translation between them","Remove the term from the glossary to avoid confusion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forcing one meaning makes outputs wrong for one department, and inference fails when context is thin. Separate contexts keep each definition precise, and explicit translation makes the mapping testable."}]},{"key":"scoping/data-access-and-permissions-discovery","sources":[],"why":"The typical stall in enterprise AI work is not model quality. It is week six of a pilot, the prototype\nworks on a folder of files someone emailed over, and the team is still waiting for a service account,\na firewall rule, a data-processing agreement or the data owner's sign-off for production access. Each of\nthose has its own approver, queue and lead time, and none of them was started on day one because nobody\nlisted them.\n\nThe second failure is worse because it is invisible. Source systems enforce permissions: a SharePoint\nsite restricted to HR, a CRM record visible only to its account team, a database row filtered by region.\nWhen you export documents into a vector index, or connect with a broad service account, those rules are\ngone. The assistant now answers anyone's question from everyone's data, which is a data leak delivered\nthrough a helpful interface. The demo cannot reveal this, because the demo user is usually an admin.\n\nThe naive approach, \"get the data first and sort out permissions later\", fails on both counts: it\ndefers the longest lead times and bakes a permission-free design into the architecture. Discovery runs\naccess and permissions as a first-class workstream, in parallel with the technical spike, so the\nprototype is built on the same access path and permission model production will use.","how":"### 1. Derive the data needs from the use case\nFrom the decomposition, list every input each node needs and every system each action touches. For\neach, ask whether it is needed to answer, to act, or only to evaluate. Fewer sources means fewer\napprovals and less exposure, *because* each source carries its own owner, contract and risk.\n\n### 2. Build a source inventory\nFor each source record: system and owner (a named person, not a team), data volume and format, freshness\nrequirement, access method (API, connector, database replica, file export, event stream), authentication\nmethod, rate limits, and whether a sandbox or non-production copy exists. Ask for a sample in week one,\n*because* samples expose format and quality problems that descriptions hide.\n\n### 3. Map the permission model at the source\nFind out how the source decides who sees what: document or item-level ACLs, inherited folder permissions,\ngroup membership from the identity provider, record ownership and sharing rules in a CRM, row-level\nsecurity in a database, or attribute-based rules (region, clearance, customer). Note how often\npermissions change and whether the source exposes them through its API, *because* an AI system can only\nenforce what it can read.\n\n### 4. Classify the data and find the legal constraints\nDetermine sensitivity (public, internal, confidential, regulated personal or health data), applicable\nregulation, contractual limits on use (for example, whether data may be sent to a third-party model\nprovider), residency requirements and retention or legal-hold obligations. These decide the deployment\nshape (provider API, private endpoint, in-customer-VPC), so they must be known before architecture is\nfixed.\n\n### 5. Choose how permissions are enforced in the AI system\nThe main options:\n- **Delegated user access**: the system calls the source with the end user's own token (for example via\n  an OAuth token-exchange or on-behalf-of flow), so the source enforces its own rules. Most faithful, but slower and bound by\n  per-user rate limits.\n- **Index with ACL metadata**: ingest content with its permission principals attached, and filter every\n  retrieval by the requesting user's resolved groups. Fast, but you must sync permission changes,\n  resolve nested groups correctly, and honour explicit deny entries where the source has them. Apply the\n  filter inside the search (pre-filtering), not to the top-k results afterwards, *because* post-filtering\n  a top-10 list for a user who can see a small share of the corpus often leaves zero results, which looks\n  like a retrieval bug rather than a permission rule.\n- **Partitioned indexes**: one index per audience (per department or tenant). Simple and robust, but\n  only works when permissions are coarse.\nEach choice depends on what step 3 found, *because* a source that does not expose ACLs through its API\nforces delegated access or coarse partitioning.\n\n### 6. Treat derived data as carrying its sources' permissions\nEmbeddings, chunk stores, cached answers, conversation memory, summaries, prompt and trace logs, and\nevaluation datasets all contain or reveal source content. Each derived item should be visible only to\nusers who could see every source it was built from, *because* a summary of an HR file and a public memo\nis as sensitive as the HR file. List where derived data lands (including the observability tool and the\nmodel provider's logs) and who can read each of those stores.\n\n### 7. Apply least privilege to actions\nFor agents that write (create tickets, update records, send email), list each action, the scope it\nneeds, and whether it acts as the user or as a service. Request read-only scopes where reading suffices\nand gate write actions behind approval.\n\n### 8. Map the approval path and lead times\nFor each source, list who must approve access (data owner, security, privacy, legal, the platform team\nthat issues credentials) and their typical turnaround. Start the slowest first and track them as project\ndependencies with owners and dates.\n\n### 9. Write permission tests early\nBefore launch, and ideally in the prototype, test that a user without access to a document cannot\nretrieve, quote or summarise it, including through indirect questions. Use real test identities with\ndiffering access.","example":"A worked scenario (lead times and counts illustrative). A legal team wants an assistant over contracts\nin SharePoint, matter data in a practice-management database, and client billing in the ERP.\n\nIn week one the FDE builds the inventory. SharePoint holds about 40,000 documents, with permissions\ninherited from matter sites, plus item-level exceptions for conflicted matters where named lawyers are\nwalled off. The practice-management database is reachable only through a nightly replica, and row-level\nvisibility is enforced by its application, not the database, so a direct database connection would see\neverything. Billing data is classified confidential, and the ERP owner says AI use requires a privacy\nreview.\n\nDecisions follow from the facts. Billing is cut from the first phase, because the use case can answer\nits target questions without it and the privacy review would dominate the timeline. Contracts are\nindexed with their ACL principals and filtered by the user's resolved groups at query time; the ethical\nwalls make permission sync freshness a hard requirement, so the team asks how quickly permission changes\nmust propagate and designs incremental ACL sync to meet it. Because a sync can still lag, documents\nfrom walled matters are also re-checked against SharePoint's live permissions at query time, before any\nof their text reaches the prompt. Matter data is read through the application's\nAPI with delegated user tokens rather than from the replica, so the application keeps enforcing its row\nrules.\n\nThe security approval for the API integration and the service registration has the longest lead time,\nso it is requested in week one. The first test written is: a lawyer walled off from a conflicted matter\nasks \"summarise the indemnity terms in the Acme agreement\" and must get no content from it.","tradeoffs":["Delegated user access keeps the source authoritative for permissions, but adds latency and per-user rate limits; when queries are high-volume and permissions are coarse, an ACL-filtered index is faster.","Indexing content with ACL metadata enables fast filtered retrieval, but makes you responsible for syncing permission changes; when walls or revocations must apply immediately, delegate instead.","Cutting sensitive sources from phase one shortens approvals dramatically, but limits what the system can answer; add them once the access pattern is proven and the review has had time to run.","Using a non-production copy speeds development, but its permissions and data quality may differ from production; when the use case depends on fine-grained ACLs, test against production permissions early."],"pitfalls":["Building the prototype on an exported folder; the symptom is a demo that delights an admin user but answers questions from documents ordinary users were never allowed to see.","Connecting with a broad service account and promising to \"add permissions later\"; the symptom is an architecture with no place to carry user identity, discovered during security review.","Ignoring permission-change latency; the symptom is a revoked or walled-off user still retrieving content for hours or days after the change, until the next full re-index.","Naming a team instead of a person as data owner; the symptom is access requests that sit unanswered for weeks because nobody feels entitled to approve them.","Securing retrieval but not the traces; the symptom is a developer or vendor support engineer able to read restricted document text in the prompt logs of the observability tool.","Treating \"the data exists\" as \"the data is usable\"; the symptom is a pipeline that connects successfully and returns scanned images, empty fields or a schema nobody can explain."],"when":"Start this in the first week of any engagement that touches customer data, which in enterprise AI is\nalmost all of them, and run it in parallel with the technical spike rather than after it. Revisit it\nwhenever a new source or action is added. It is non-negotiable for retrieval over internal documents,\nfor agents with write access, and for regulated data. Measure it by whether every source has a named\nowner, an access method, a permission model, a classification and an approval with a date, and whether\npermission tests exist and pass. In interviews, \"how would you build RAG over the company's SharePoint\"\nis often really a question about this topic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements identity propagation and ACL filtering on retrieval, and writes automated permission tests with distinct test identities that run on every index change.","architect":"Chooses delegated access, ACL-filtered indexing or partitioning per source, and sets the deployment boundary that the data classification and residency rules require.","fde":"Finds the real data owners, files and chases access requests from week one, and surfaces permission models and lead times that would otherwise stall the pilot."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for a company-wide assistant over their document management system and offers a\nservice account with read access to everything \"to get started quickly\". How do you respond?","a":"I would accept it only for a sandbox with non-sensitive content, and not as the production design.\nA read-everything account erases the source's permissions, so every user could retrieve anything the\naccount sees. Instead I would find out how the system expresses permissions (item ACLs, inherited site\npermissions, identity-provider groups) and whether its API exposes them, then either ingest with ACL\nprincipals and filter by the user's resolved groups or call the source with delegated user tokens. I\nwould also ask how quickly revocations must propagate, classify which content is sensitive, start the\napprovals now, and write a test proving a user cannot retrieve a document they lack access to."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a prototype built on exported documents risky even if it performs well?","options":["Exported documents are usually lower resolution than the originals","The export drops the source's permissions, so the design never accounts for who may see what","Exports cannot be embedded by most embedding models","Exported documents are always out of date"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exports and broad service accounts discard ACLs. The prototype then demonstrates answers that ordinary users should never get, and the architecture has no place to carry user identity into retrieval."},{"q":"A source system enforces row-level visibility in its application layer, not in its database. What does that imply for the AI integration?","options":["A read replica of the database is the safest integration point","Row-level rules can be ignored because the database has no restrictions","Reading the database directly bypasses the rules, so access should go through the application's API with user context","The data must be copied into a separate warehouse first"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the application enforces visibility, the database itself returns every row to any connection. Going through the application's API as the user keeps its rules in force; a direct replica bypasses them."},{"q":"Why start access and approval requests in the first week rather than after the prototype proves value?","options":["Approvers prefer to see requests before any code exists","Early requests are cheaper because licences cost less","Approvals often have the longest lead times, and the access path chosen shapes the architecture","The prototype cannot run without production credentials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Security, privacy and owner sign-offs run on their own queues. Deferring them adds their full lead time after the prototype, and the access method (delegated, ACL index, partitioned) changes the design."}]},{"key":"scoping/success-metrics-kpis","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"AI projects rarely fail in the middle. They fail at the review meeting. The demo impressed, the pilot\nran for six weeks, and then the sponsor asks \"so, did it work?\" and nobody had agreed what \"work\" meant.\nEngineers bring the numbers they can measure: 94% answer correctness on a golden set, p95 latency of\n3 seconds. The sponsor needs a different kind of number: cost per resolved case, cycle time, revenue,\nregulatory exposure. Nothing in the room connects the first set to the second, so the project gets\njudged on anecdote. That might be the one embarrassing answer someone screenshotted.\n\nThe naive alternatives each break in a predictable way. \"We'll know it when we see it\" leaves the\nverdict to recency bias, and the goalposts move: once accuracy is good, the question becomes adoption,\nand once adoption is good, it becomes cost. Picking one metric invites Goodhart's law (a measure that\nbecomes a target stops being a good measure). A support bot's deflection rate climbs fastest when the\nbot makes it hard to reach a human. Reporting only model metrics leaves the project defenceless at\nbudget time, because nobody can translate \"faithfulness 0.91\" into money. And metrics chosen *after*\ndeployment cannot be falsified, because people pick whichever one looks good. Defining metrics up front\nis what makes the project a testable claim instead of a story.","how":"### Build a metric tree, not a list\nArrange metrics in levels connected by a stated causal hypothesis:\n\n1. **Outcome KPI** (lagging, monthly or quarterly). This is what the sponsor reports upward: cost per\n   resolved ticket, claim cycle time, days sales outstanding, analyst hours per report. Keep it to one\n   or at most two. Several outcome KPIs dilute accountability.\n2. **Operational drivers** (leading, weekly). These are the behaviours that should move the outcome:\n   share of cases completed without a human, handle time on AI-assisted cases, adoption rate (share of\n   eligible work where the tool was actually used), edit rate on drafts.\n3. **System quality** (daily, from evals and logs). This is what the engineering team controls directly:\n   task completion rate, extraction field accuracy, retrieval recall, escalation precision, p95 latency,\n   cost per successful task.\n4. **Guardrails** (counter-metrics). These must not get worse while the others improve: customer\n   satisfaction, reopen rate, compliance errors, misrouted high-severity cases.\n\nThe tree exists because outcome KPIs move slowly and are confounded by seasonality, staffing and pricing\nchanges, so you cannot steer week to week by them. Leading indicators predict them. Writing the causal\nlinks down also makes failures diagnosable: if level 3 improves but level 1 does not, one specific link\nin the hypothesis is wrong, and you can see which one.\n\n### Write a spec for every metric\nEach metric gets a spec: the name, the exact numerator and denominator, the population (which cases,\nwith what exclusions), the data source and who owns that pipeline, the measurement window, the baseline\nvalue and how it was measured, the target, and a named owner. This is tedious *because* it is the part\nthat gets disputed. In three departments \"resolved\" can mean three things: closed by an agent, no\ncustomer reply for 72 hours, or not reopened within 7 days. The denominator matters just as much. If\nyou drop \"out-of-scope\" tickets after the fact, the automation rate inflates silently.\n\n### Turn targets into decision rules\nA target needs a reference point, a horizon and a consequence. For example: \"median cycle time falls\nfrom the measured baseline of X to at most Y within 90 days of pilot start. If it does, we expand to\nregion B. If it improves by less than Z, we stop.\" Without a pre-agreed decision rule the target is\ndecoration, and the review turns into a negotiation.\n\n### Pair every target with its cheapest way to cheat\nFor each metric you optimise, name the cheap way to game it and add a guardrail that would catch it.\nDeflection pairs with reopen rate and escalations within 24 hours. Handle time pairs with a QA score.\nAuto-approval rate pairs with the error rate on auto-approved items, audited by sampling. This does not\nassume bad faith. Optimisation pressure, including the team's own prompt iteration against the metric, finds the\ncheap path by default.\n\n### Check the target is attributable and detectable\nA target is only useful if the pilot can tell whether it was hit, and that takes two more decisions\nmade now, not at the review. **Attribution:** what would the KPI have done without the system?\nOutcome KPIs drift with seasonality, staffing and pricing, so agree the comparison up front: a\nconcurrent holdout group, a staggered rollout, or at minimum a before/after read against a comparison\nqueue (the baseline lesson covers these designs). **Detectability:** a rule of thumb from online\nexperimentation is n ≈ 16σ²/δ² cases per group for about 80% power at a two-sided 5% significance\nlevel, where δ is the smallest change that matters and, for a rate, σ² = p(1−p). Guardrails are where\nthis bites, because they are rare-event rates. Detecting a misrouting rise from 1.5% to 2.5% needs about\n16 × 0.015 × 0.985 / 0.01² ≈ 2,400 cases per group. If the pilot will not see that volume, lengthen the\nwindow, accept a wider tolerance, or back the guardrail with targeted audits instead of the raw rate.","example":"Here is a worked scenario with illustrative numbers. An insurer wants an assistant that reads\nfirst-notice-of-loss (FNOL) emails, extracts policy number, loss date, loss type and description, and\npre-fills the claim record for an adjuster to confirm.\n\n- **Outcome KPI:** median hours from FNOL receipt to claim set up. The baseline over 8 weeks was\n  26 hours. The target is at most 8 hours, 90 days after the pilot starts.\n- **Driver:** the share of FNOLs where every mandatory field was pre-filled and the adjuster accepted\n  it without edits. The target is 70%.\n- **System quality:** field-level accuracy on a 400-email golden set. The target is at least 99% for\n  policy number and at least 90% for loss-type category, measured on every prompt or model change.\n- **Guardrails:** the misrouting rate (claim sent to the wrong line of business) stays at or below the\n  1.5% baseline, and no claim is set up against the wrong policy number, with weekly audits.\n\nIn week 3 the driver reads 78%, ahead of target, and cycle time has fallen to 11 hours. The guardrail\nshows misrouting at 3.1%. Is that noise? With about 700 FNOLs that week (illustrative), 3.1% is roughly\n22 misroutes against about 10 expected at the 1.5% baseline, and a count of 10 varies by only about ±3\nweek to week, so this is a real shift, not a bad draw. A drill-down finds that adjusters are accepting pre-filled loss types without\nreading them on the busiest queue, and that the extractor confuses \"escape of water\" with \"flood\" in\none region's phrasing. Without the guardrail, the dashboard would have said \"success\" while claims\nwent to the wrong handlers. The team disables auto-accept for the water categories, adds 40\nregion-specific emails to the golden set, and re-runs the eval. The tree told them which link broke: the\ndriver rose for the wrong reason.","tradeoffs":["More metrics give better diagnosis but blur accountability. Keep one outcome KPI and three to five drivers, and add more only if a review cannot locate a failure.","Outcome KPIs are what sponsors care about, but they lag by weeks and carry confounders. Lean on leading drivers for steering unless the outcome moves fast, as in real-time conversion.","Tight metric specs prevent disputes but take days of negotiation with data owners. For a throwaway proof of concept, a looser spec is acceptable if it is labelled as such.","Ambitious targets motivate, but they make a sound project look like a failure. Set targets from a measured baseline and the realistic human error rate, not from vendor marketing.","Hard guardrails (a breach stops rollout) protect users but can halt a sound launch over noise. Define the breach threshold with a confidence interval, not a single week's reading."],"pitfalls":["Choosing model metrics as the success criteria. The symptom is a project that hits 95% accuracy and still gets cancelled because nobody could say what it saved.","Leaving the denominator undefined. The symptom is two teams reporting different automation rates from the same data and the review turning into an argument about definitions.","Setting targets before measuring the baseline. The symptom is a demand for 99% accuracy on a task where the human team, once audited, turns out to be 88% accurate.","No guardrail for the optimised metric. The symptom is the headline number improving while complaints, reopens or downstream rework quietly rise.","Measuring adoption as logins rather than use on eligible work. The symptom is high \"active users\" alongside no change in handle time."],"when":"Define success metrics in the first two weeks of any customer-facing AI engagement, before\narchitecture choices, because the metrics decide what you build: a cycle-time KPI favours automation,\nwhile a quality KPI favours assistive drafting with review. Skip the full tree only for internal\nspikes whose sole question is technical feasibility, and say so in writing. In day-to-day work the tree\nbecomes the pilot dashboard and the agenda of every steering meeting. In interviews, expect \"how would\nyou measure success for X?\" A strong answer names the outcome, the drivers, the guardrail that catches\ngaming, and how the baseline gets measured.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the system so every driver and quality metric can be computed from logs, with task IDs that join model events to business records.","architect":"Owns the causal hypothesis linking system quality to business outcome, and makes sure the platform can attribute outcomes to a specific model and prompt version.","fde":"Negotiates the metric specs with the customer's operations and finance owners, and gets the decision rule signed before the pilot starts."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks you to \"maximise the share of chats resolved by the bot.\" Why is that a dangerous\nsole KPI, and what would you propose instead?","a":"Bot-resolved share rises if the bot makes escalation hard, ends chats early, or answers\nconfidently when it should hand off, so it rewards exactly the failures that hurt customers. I would\nmake the outcome KPI cost per *successfully* resolved contact, or contacts per customer per month.\nBot resolution share would be one driver, defined as no human contact and no repeat contact on the same\nissue within 7 days. Guardrails would be CSAT on bot-resolved chats, the 7-day repeat-contact rate, and\nthe share of high-severity intents escalated within one turn. I would measure all of these over a\nbaseline window first, and agree in writing what result triggers expansion or a stop."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a metric tree separate operational drivers from the outcome KPI?","options":["Drivers are easier for executives to understand than outcomes","Outcomes lag and are confounded, so leading drivers are needed to steer and to locate which causal link failed","Drivers replace the outcome KPI once the pilot starts","Outcome KPIs cannot be measured for AI systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outcome KPIs such as cost per case move slowly and depend on seasonality and staffing. Drivers move weekly and predict the outcome. Keeping both, linked by a stated hypothesis, shows whether a missed outcome came from low adoption, poor quality, or a wrong assumption."},{"q":"A deflection bot's containment rate rose from 40% to 65% in a month. What should you check first?","options":["Whether the model was upgraded to a larger version","Whether latency improved enough to explain it","Guardrails such as repeat contacts and escalations within a few days, which reveal contained-but-unresolved chats","Whether the golden-set accuracy also rose by 25 points"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Containment is easy to game, even unintentionally, by making handoff harder. The repeat-contact and reopen guardrails show whether contained chats were actually resolved. Accuracy and latency changes do not answer that question."},{"q":"What makes a success target usable at a pilot review?","options":["It is ambitious enough to impress the sponsor","It is expressed as a model metric so engineering can own it","It is chosen after seeing the first month of pilot data","It has a measured baseline, a horizon and a pre-agreed decision such as expand or stop"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A target becomes a decision tool only when it has a reference point, a deadline and an agreed consequence. Targets chosen after seeing data cannot be falsified, and model metrics alone do not show business value."}]},{"key":"scoping/baseline-measurement-before-deployment","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"Every claim of AI impact is a difference: handle time fell, errors dropped, throughput rose. A\ndifference needs two numbers, and the first one has to be measured before deployment, because\ndeployment destroys it. Once the assistant is live, agents work differently, the old tool gets\nswitched off, the team is reorganised around the new flow, and the historical data was never logged\nat the right granularity. Trying to reconstruct the baseline at review time produces exactly the\ncontested, cherry-picked number the review was meant to avoid.\n\nThe naive substitutes fail in known ways. Existing management reports use their own definitions: they\noften exclude escalated cases, mix business days with calendar days, or report a target rather than a\nmeasurement. Asking subject-matter experts to estimate is unreliable, because people tend to misjudge\nhow long their own work takes and how often they make mistakes. That is why direct measurement\nbeats self-report. The most expensive failure is skipping the *quality* baseline. Customers often\ndemand near-perfect accuracy from the AI while the human process, once anyone checks, makes errors on\na meaningful fraction of cases. If you have not measured human error the same way, you will be held\nto an imaginary standard. The project can then fail a bar the incumbent process never met.","how":"### 1. Start from the metric spec\nBaseline exactly the metrics in the agreed spec, with the same numerator, denominator, population and\nexclusions you will use after launch. If the definitions differ between before and after, the\ncomparison is invalid, however careful the measurement was.\n\n### 2. Define the unit of work and the window\nPick the unit you will count: a ticket, a claim, a document, a report. Then choose a window long\nenough to cover the work's natural cycles, such as month-end peaks, weekly patterns and seasonal\nproducts. A two-week baseline taken in a quiet month and compared against a peak-season pilot will\nshow a \"regression\" that is really just volume.\n\n### 3. Choose measurement methods per metric\n- **System timestamps** from the ticketing, case-management or workflow tool, for cycle time and\n  throughput. They are cheap and complete, but check what each timestamp actually records.\n- **Time-and-motion or work sampling** for effort that the system does not log, such as time spent\n  searching other tools. This is observational and small-sample, but it is the only way to see hidden work.\n- **Retrospective quality audit:** SMEs blind re-review a random sample of past human outputs against\n  the same rubric the AI will face. This is the step teams skip, and it is the one that sets a fair bar.\n- **Shadow labelling:** humans keep working normally while the system's proposed output is logged\n  but not used. This gives a paired, same-case comparison.\n\n### 4. Capture distribution, not just averages\nRecord the median, p90 and standard deviation, and keep the raw per-case data. Variance determines how\nmany post-launch cases you need to detect a change. Handle times are usually right-skewed, so medians\nand percentiles are more honest than means, and a log transform helps when testing.\n\n### 5. Stratify by case type\nReport each metric per segment (category, channel, region, complexity). This matters *because* of mix\nshift. If the AI pilot happens to receive easier cases, the overall number improves even when no\nsegment does. This is a composition (mix) effect; its extreme form, where the aggregate trend runs\nopposite to every segment's trend, is Simpson's paradox. Comparing within segments and re-weighting to\nthe baseline mix removes that illusion.\n\n### 6. Size the comparison\nFor a proportion measured to within ±e, you need roughly n ≈ z²·p(1−p)/e² cases. To detect a mean\nshift δ between two groups with standard deviation σ, you need roughly\nn ≈ 2(z_α/2 + z_β)²σ²/δ² per arm. If the pilot cannot reach that volume in its window, you learn\nthat before launch, not at the review.\n\n### 7. Freeze it\nStore the queries, the data snapshot, the definitions, the date range, and the names of the people who\nsigned off. A baseline that nobody can re-run is just an anecdote with a decimal point.\n\n### Choosing the after-comparison design\nThe baseline also determines how \"after\" will be read. A simple before/after comparison is weakest\nbecause it absorbs seasonality and any concurrent change. A concurrent holdout (some teams or queues\nwithout the tool) controls for time. A staggered rollout gives you both. Shadow mode gives paired\ncomparisons on identical cases, but it measures quality, not the behavioural effect on humans.\n\nWith a holdout, the estimate is a difference-in-differences:\n\n```\neffect = (pilot_after − pilot_before) − (holdout_after − holdout_before)\n```\n\nThe second bracket subtracts whatever happened to everyone during the pilot, such as a seasonal dip, a\npolicy change or a new-hire cohort. That is why the holdout needs its own baseline over the same window,\nnot just a post-launch reading. The design assumes both groups would have moved in parallel without the\ntool, so check that their pre-period trends really do look parallel before trusting the number.","example":"A support team will pilot an AI drafting assistant. This script computes the per-category baseline\nfrom an 8-week ticket export, including a confidence interval on the human error rate from an SME audit\nsample, and the pilot volume needed to detect a 2-minute drop in handle time.\n\n```python\nimport csv, math, statistics as st\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef wilson(k, n, z=1.96):\n    \"\"\"95% Wilson score interval for a proportion k/n (well-behaved for small n).\"\"\"\n    p, d = k / n, 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / d\n    half = z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / d\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\ndef n_per_arm(sd, delta, z_a=1.96, z_b=0.84):\n    \"\"\"Cases per arm to detect a mean shift of `delta` (two-sided 5%, 80% power).\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(2 * (z_a + z_b) ** 2 * sd ** 2 / delta ** 2)\n\nby_cat = defaultdict(list)\nwith open(\"tickets_w01_w08.csv\") as f:          # 8-week export, one row per closed ticket\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        by_cat[r[\"category\"]].append(\n            (float(r[\"handle_min\"]), r[\"audited\"] == \"1\", r[\"audit_error\"] == \"1\"))\n\nfor cat, rows in sorted(by_cat.items()):\n    mins = [m for m, _, _ in rows]\n    errs = [e for _, audited, e in rows if audited]   # SME blind re-review sample\n    lo, hi = wilson(sum(errs), len(errs)) if errs else (float(\"nan\"),) * 2\n    print(f\"{cat:10} n={len(mins):5} median={st.median(mins):5.1f} \"\n          f\"p90={st.quantiles(mins, n=10)[8]:5.1f} sd={st.stdev(mins):5.1f} \"\n          f\"audited={len(errs):4} error_rate_95ci=[{lo:.1%}, {hi:.1%}]\")\n\nprint(n_per_arm(sd=9.0, delta=2.0))   # billing: sd 9 min, detect a 2-minute drop -> 318\n```\n\nRead the output critically. Suppose billing shows 11 errors in 120 audited tickets. The point estimate\nis 9.2%, but the Wilson interval is roughly 5% to 16%. \"The AI must beat 9.2%\" is therefore false\nprecision. The honest bar is \"not worse than the human interval\", and you may need to enlarge the\naudit before signing it. The sample-size line says the pilot needs about 318 billing tickets in each\narm. If billing only gets 80 tickets a week, a two-week pilot cannot answer the question.","tradeoffs":["Longer baseline windows capture seasonality and reduce noise but delay the project. Shorten them only when the work has no strong cycles and volume is high enough to reach the needed sample size.","Retrospective quality audits cost SME hours, which the customer often resists. They become non-negotiable when the success criterion compares AI quality to human quality, which is most of the time.","Concurrent holdout groups give the cleanest causal read but withhold the tool from some users. Use a pure before/after comparison only when the expected effect is far larger than seasonal variation.","Shadow mode gives paired same-case comparisons without risk, but it does not measure how humans change when they actually rely on the output, so it cannot replace a live comparison.","Heavy stratification makes comparisons fair but splits the sample thin. Merge segments whose baselines are statistically indistinguishable."],"pitfalls":["Using management-report numbers as the baseline. The symptom is a \"before\" figure nobody can reproduce from raw data, and a dispute at review about what it included.","Baselining only speed and not quality. The symptom is the customer holding the AI to a near-perfect accuracy bar the human process was never measured against.","Ignoring case mix. The symptom is an overall improvement that disappears when results are broken down by category, because the pilot queue received easier work.","Changing the metric definition after launch, for example by excluding a newly \"out of scope\" case type. The symptom is an improvement that appears exactly when the definition changed.","Announcing the measurement to the team being measured, then using that period as the baseline. The symptom is unusually good baseline numbers (an observer, or Hawthorne, effect) that regress once observation ends, which makes the AI look worse than it is.","Picking the pilot team or queue because its recent numbers are the worst. The symptom is a large \"improvement\" that the holdout, or the same team's longer history, shows would have happened anyway, because an unusually bad period tends to be followed by a more ordinary one (regression to the mean)."],"when":"Measure a baseline for every metric in the success spec before any pilot user sees the system,\nideally during discovery while you are already mapping the workflow. If historical logs are good, the\nbaseline can be largely retrospective and take days. If effort or quality is not logged, plan two to\nfour weeks of sampling and audits, and put that in the statement of work as a customer dependency.\nSkip it only for pure feasibility spikes with no value claim. In interviews, a baseline question usually\nhides inside \"how would you prove the pilot worked?\" The expected answer covers before/after\ndefinitions, human error rates, mix shift and sample size.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the extraction queries and audit-sampling scripts, and makes the post-launch telemetry compute metrics with exactly the same definitions as the baseline.","architect":"Chooses the comparison design (holdout, staggered rollout, shadow mode) and makes sure the platform can route and tag cases so that design is possible.","fde":"Gets access to historical exports, schedules SME audit time, and gets the customer to sign off on the frozen baseline before the pilot starts."},"check":{"q":"The customer wants the AI to reach 99% accuracy before rollout. You have no baseline for the human\nteam. What do you do, and why?","a":"I would run a blind retrospective audit. SMEs re-review a random, stratified sample of recent\nhuman outputs against the same rubric we will use for the AI. The sample should be large enough that\nthe error-rate interval is narrow enough to set a bar. This changes the conversation from an\naspirational number to a fair comparison. If humans turn out to be at, say, 90% to 94%, a target of \"at\nleast as good as humans, with no regression on high-severity case types\" is defensible. The 99% figure\ncan stay as a stretch goal for low-risk categories. I would also record per-category human rates,\nbecause the AI may beat humans on routine work and lose on rare, complex cases. Those rare cases are\nwhere review should stay mandatory."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the baseline be measured before deployment rather than reconstructed afterwards?","options":["Historical data is always deleted when new software is installed","Deployment changes the process and people's behaviour, and old logs rarely match the new metric definitions","Statistical tests require the baseline to be collected first chronologically","Regulators require baselines to be dated before a go-live"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Once the system is live, the old process is gone and behaviour has shifted, so it cannot be re-observed. Historical reports usually use different definitions, which makes a reconstructed baseline contestable."},{"q":"A pilot shows a 20% drop in average handle time overall, but no individual ticket category improved. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The confidence interval is too wide to tell","The model improved only on the largest category","The pilot received a different mix of case types, with more easy cases, than the baseline","Handle time should have been measured with medians instead of means"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If no segment improved but the total did, the weights changed: this is mix shift, the composition effect behind Simpson's paradox. Stratifying the baseline and re-weighting pilot results to the baseline mix exposes it. Medians would not fix a composition change."},{"q":"Why record the standard deviation of a baseline metric, not just its mean?","options":["Executives prefer dashboards that show variability","The standard deviation is needed to compute the ROI","It replaces the need to stratify by case type","It determines how many post-launch cases are needed to detect a given improvement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Required sample size scales with the variance divided by the square of the effect you want to detect. Without the spread, you cannot tell whether the pilot window has enough volume to distinguish a real change from noise."}]},{"key":"scoping/roi-model","sources":[],"why":"AI projects compete for budget against every other project, and the person who approves the budget\nreasons in payback periods and net present value, not in eval scores. If you cannot say what the\nsystem is worth, someone else will decide for you, usually with a vendor's slide or a gut feeling.\nThe model also protects the customer: a use case that cannot clear break-even under plausible\nassumptions should be found in week two, not in month nine.\n\nThe naive version is a one-line spreadsheet: \"agents spend 12 minutes per ticket, AI saves 50%,\n40,000 tickets a month, so we save 240,000 minutes.\" It fails in four ways. It treats freed time as\nmoney, but salaries do not fall when each person saves twenty minutes a day unless hiring is avoided,\novertime drops, or the time goes to work that has value. It assumes full adoption from day one, when\nreal adoption ramps and plateaus below 100%. It ignores the recurring costs AI adds: inference per\ntask (including failed and retried tasks), human review of outputs, eval-set upkeep, monitoring, and\nre-validation when a model version is deprecated. And it presents a point estimate. A point estimate\nhides which assumption the whole case rests on, so no one knows what to measure in the pilot to\nconfirm or kill it.","how":"### Benefit lines\nBuild each benefit as a product of measurable factors, so every factor can be traced to a baseline or\na pilot measurement:\n\n- **Labour efficiency** = eligible volume × adoption × time saved per used case × loaded cost per\n  minute × capture rate. The *capture rate* is the share of freed time that turns into cash or\n  redeployed capacity, and finance should own that number. It is high when the team is growing (hires\n  avoided), when there is overtime or contractor spend, or when there is a backlog with a cost. It is\n  low when the freed time just becomes slack.\n- **Quality / error avoidance** = volume × change in error rate × cost per error (rework, credits,\n  write-offs, penalties). This only counts if the error rate was baselined.\n- **Revenue** = affected volume × change in conversion or speed × value per unit. This is the hardest\n  line to attribute, so it needs a holdout group to defend.\n- **Risk reduction** = change in probability × impact of a loss event. Present it separately, because\n  finance discounts it heavily.\n\n### Cost lines\n- **One-time:** discovery, integration, data preparation, building the eval set (SME hours count),\n  security review, training and change management.\n- **Recurring:** inference cost per task × total tasks attempted, platform or vendor fees, human\n  review minutes per output × loaded cost, engineering upkeep (prompt and eval maintenance,\n  monitoring, incident response), and periodic re-validation when the provider retires a model.\n\nDivide recurring cost by *successful* tasks, not attempted ones. Retries and failed attempts consume\ntokens but produce nothing, and the review time on a bad draft is a cost, not a saving.\n\n### Compare against the next-best alternative\nThe counterfactual is rarely \"do nothing\". A macro library, a form redesign, a rules engine or a\nprocess change might capture part of the same benefit at a fraction of the cost and risk, and a sharp\nfinance reviewer will ask about it. State the AI case as incremental value over the next-best\nalternative. If better templates would remove half of the handle time the assistant targets, the AI\nproject's return is computed on the other half, and its build cost is compared with the template\nproject's cost, not with zero.\n\n### Horizon, ramp and summary measures\nModel month by month over a horizon the sponsor uses, often 24 or 36 months, with an adoption ramp\ntaken from comparable rollouts or the pilot. Report three numbers, because they answer different\nquestions:\n- **ROI** = (total benefit − total cost) / total cost.\n- **Payback month**, the month cumulative net value turns positive, which is the cash-flow question.\n- **NPV**, net value discounted at the customer's rate, for comparing against other investments.\n\n### Sensitivity and break-even\nVary one input at a time across its plausible range and rank the inputs by how far they swing NPV.\nThis is a tornado analysis. Then solve for the break-even value of the most uncertain input. The model\nexists to answer this question. If break-even needs 2 minutes saved and the pilot measures 4, the\ncase is robust. If it needs 3.8, the pilot has to be designed to measure that one number precisely.\n\nOne-at-a-time sensitivity assumes the inputs move independently, and in AI rollouts they often do not.\nLow minutes saved and low adoption tend to arrive together, because people abandon a tool that saves\nthem little. So also run a joint pessimistic scenario, or a simple Monte Carlo over the input ranges,\nand report the chance that NPV is negative alongside the tornado chart.","example":"A drafting assistant for a 40,000-ticket-per-month support team. All inputs are illustrative. A real\nmodel takes volume and handle time from the baseline, loaded cost and capture rate from finance, and AI\ncost per ticket from a token measurement on real traffic.\n\n```python\nBASE = dict(tickets=40_000, min_saved=4.0, cost_per_min=0.80, capture=0.6,\n            adoption_ramp=[0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7], adoption_steady=0.7,\n            ai_cost_per_ticket=0.04, review_min=0.0, platform=6_000, upkeep=10_000,\n            build=180_000)                  # review_min=0: min_saved is measured net of review\n\ndef monthly(p, month):\n    ramp = p[\"adoption_ramp\"][month] if month < len(p[\"adoption_ramp\"]) else 1.0\n    a = min(ramp, p[\"adoption_steady\"])          # the plateau caps the ramp\n    benefit = p[\"tickets\"] * a * (p[\"min_saved\"] - p[\"review_min\"]) * p[\"cost_per_min\"] * p[\"capture\"]\n    run = p[\"tickets\"] * p[\"ai_cost_per_ticket\"] + p[\"platform\"] + p[\"upkeep\"]  # drafts for every ticket\n    return benefit, run\n\ndef evaluate(p, months=24, rate=0.10):\n    cum, payback, npv = -p[\"build\"], None, -p[\"build\"]\n    for m in range(months):\n        b, r = monthly(p, m)\n        cum += b - r\n        npv += (b - r) / (1 + rate / 12) ** (m + 1)\n        if payback is None and cum >= 0:\n            payback = m + 1\n    benefit = sum(monthly(p, m)[0] for m in range(months))\n    cost = p[\"build\"] + sum(monthly(p, m)[1] for m in range(months))\n    return dict(roi=(benefit - cost) / cost, payback=payback, npv=round(npv))\n\nprint(\"base\", evaluate(BASE))                      # ROI ~ +100%, payback month 8, NPV ~ 517k\nfor k, v in [(\"capture\", 0.3), (\"adoption_steady\", 0.4), (\"min_saved\", 2.5), (\"upkeep\", 20_000)]:\n    print(k, v, evaluate({**BASE, k: v}))\n# capture 0.3 -> ROI ~ 0, no payback in 24 months; adoption 0.4 -> +19%, month 16\n# min_saved 2.5 -> +25%, month 15;               upkeep 20k -> +43%, month 11\n\nlo, hi = 0.0, 10.0                                 # break-even on minutes saved\nfor _ in range(40):\n    mid = (lo + hi) / 2\n    lo, hi = (mid, hi) if evaluate({**BASE, \"min_saved\": mid})[\"roi\"] < 0 else (lo, mid)\nprint(f\"break-even minutes saved: {hi:.2f}\")       # ~2.0\n```\n\nThe base case looks strong. The sensitivity run shows that halving the capture rate wipes out the\nreturn entirely, while doubling engineering upkeep roughly halves it but leaves it clearly positive. So the most valuable scoping\nconversation is with finance about what happens to freed time, not with engineering about tokens.\nThe pilot must also measure minutes saved precisely, because the case survives anything above about\n2 minutes. Finally, the one-at-a-time view flatters the case. A joint scenario that is only mildly\npessimistic on each input (capture 0.45, adoption 0.5, 3 minutes saved, each milder than its\nsingle-input stress above) gives an ROI of about −17% and no payback within 24 months. Three modest\ndisappointments together sink a case that survives each one alone.","tradeoffs":["Ranges and sensitivity analysis are more honest than a point estimate, but sponsors often want one number. Lead with the conservative case, and show the range alongside it.","Including risk-reduction and revenue benefits raises the headline, but those lines are hard to attribute. Keep them separate unless a holdout group can measure them.","A longer horizon makes payback look easier, but it assumes model costs, prices and the workflow stay stable. Use 24 months or less when the provider or the process is likely to change.","A detailed bottom-up model is defensible but slow to build. For an early go/no-go, a 10-line version with honest ranges is enough, provided the break-even input is identified.","Counting capture rate at 100% simplifies the story but overstates value. It is only justified when the team is actively hiring or paying for overtime the tool will remove."],"pitfalls":["Treating time saved as cash saved. The symptom is a CFO asking which budget line goes down, and nobody being able to answer.","Pricing inference on successful tasks only. The symptom is actual monthly spend running well above the model, because retries, failed runs and unused drafts were never counted.","Assuming instant, complete adoption. The symptom is a payback month that slips every quarter while usage sits on a plateau far below the modelled figure.","Forgetting the human review cost. The symptom is \"time saved\" shrinking in the pilot because reviewers spend minutes checking each output.","Comparing the AI case against doing nothing. The symptom is a finance reviewer asking why a cheaper process or template fix was not costed, and the model having no answer.","Building the model before the baseline exists. The symptom is ROI inputs that the customer's own data contradicts when the pilot report is reviewed."],"when":"Build a first-cut ROI model during scoping, as soon as the success metrics are agreed. Use it to kill\nor prioritise use cases before anyone writes integration code. Refine it with baseline numbers, then\nwith pilot measurements, and re-run it before any expansion decision. Skip it only for exploratory\nwork explicitly funded as learning. In practice the most useful outputs are the break-even value and\nthe ranked sensitivity list, because they tell you which number the pilot must measure. In interviews,\nexpect \"this use case saves 50% of time, what is it worth?\" The strong answer asks about capture rate,\nadoption, review cost and cost per successful task before multiplying.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures real cost per successful task from traces, including retries and failures, and supplies the inference and upkeep numbers the model needs.","architect":"Owns the cost structure behind the model: model tier, caching, review design and hosting choices, which set recurring cost and therefore break-even.","fde":"Builds the model with the customer's finance and operations owners, so capture rate and loaded cost are their numbers, which makes the result credible internally."},"check":{"q":"A pilot shows a drafting tool saves 5 minutes per ticket, but the sponsor's finance team rejects\nthe ROI case. What are the likely reasons, and how do you fix the model?","a":"Most likely the model counted all freed time as cash. If the team is not shrinking or avoiding\nhires, those minutes do not reduce any budget line, so the capture rate is far below 100%. Other\nlikely gaps: the 5 minutes was measured on the cases where the tool was used, not across all eligible\nwork at realistic adoption; review time on bad drafts was not subtracted; and inference was priced per\nsuccessful draft instead of per attempt. I would rebuild with finance's own capture assumption, which\nmight be avoided hiring next year, overtime or backlog cost. I would use the measured adoption curve\nand full per-attempt cost, then show the break-even capture rate so they can judge whether it is plausible."},"quiz":[{"q":"In an AI labour-efficiency benefit, what does the capture rate represent?","options":["The share of tickets the model handles without a human","The share of freed employee time that turns into real savings or valuable redeployed capacity","The fraction of model outputs that pass the eval suite","The share of users who adopt the tool after training"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Saving minutes reduces cost only if hiring, overtime or contractor spend falls, or if the time goes to valuable work. Capture rate encodes that, and it often swings ROI more than accuracy does. Adoption and automation rate are separate factors."},{"q":"Why should recurring AI cost be divided by successful tasks rather than attempted tasks?","options":["Providers only bill for successful completions","Attempted tasks are hard to count in logs","Failed attempts and retries consume tokens and review time but deliver no value, so they belong in the cost of each success","It makes the cost per task look smaller to the sponsor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per successful task is the true unit cost. Failures, retries and drafts that get discarded are paid for but produce nothing. Dividing by attempts understates the cost of the value you actually deliver."},{"q":"A sensitivity analysis shows NPV swings most with minutes saved per case. What should the scoping team do?","options":["Raise the assumed minutes saved to give the case more margin","Drop the sensitivity analysis and present the base case","Shorten the horizon so the assumption matters less","Compute break-even minutes saved and design the pilot to measure that input precisely"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The highest-sensitivity input decides the investment, so the pilot should measure it against the break-even value. Raising the assumption hides the risk. Shortening the horizon changes the question without reducing the uncertainty."}]},{"key":"scoping/statement-of-work","sources":[],"why":"Two parties start a project with different pictures of the finish line. The customer imagines \"an\nassistant that answers our policy questions correctly.\" The vendor or internal team imagines \"a RAG\nprototype over the documents you give us.\" Both pictures are reasonable, and the gap stays invisible\nuntil the delivery date, when it becomes a dispute about money and reputation. The SOW makes the\npictures match in writing while changing them is still cheap.\n\nGeneric software SOWs fail on AI work in specific ways. \"The system shall answer questions correctly\"\ncannot be accepted or rejected, because any probabilistic system answers some questions wrongly, so the\ncustomer can always find a failure. \"Customer will provide data\" with no date or format turns a\nthree-week data delay into the delivery team's missed milestone. A fixed-scope, fixed-price commitment\nto a quality level nobody has measured on the customer's data transfers unpriced research risk to\nwhoever signed it. And when nothing says who owns the eval set, prompts and fine-tuned weights, or who\noperates the system after go-live, those questions come up at the worst moment. The SOW exists to settle\nthese questions before work starts, while each side can still walk away.","how":"### Where it sits\nCommercial engagements usually have a master services agreement (MSA) holding the legal terms, such\nas liability, confidentiality, IP defaults and payment. Each project then gets an SOW under it with the\nspecifics. An order-of-precedence clause says which document wins when they conflict. Internal projects often\nhave a charter or project brief that does the same job. The\nstructure below applies to both. The legal terms are for counsel, and this lesson is not legal advice.\n\n### Core sections\n1. **Objective and success metrics.** The business problem and the agreed metric tree, so every\n   deliverable can be traced to a purpose.\n2. **Scope and deliverables.** These are concrete artefacts: a deployed service in a named\n   environment, an eval report, a runbook, a handover session. Name the use cases, document types,\n   languages and channels that are in scope.\n3. **Out of scope.** This is explicit, because scope disputes are almost always about what was\n   *assumed* to be included: other languages, write-back to the CRM, mobile, 24/7 support.\n4. **Acceptance criteria.** Each deliverable has an observable test, and for model behaviour that test\n   is statistical. \"On the agreed held-out eval set of N cases, field accuracy is at least X% overall\n   and at least Y% on each named critical field, with no critical-severity failures, measured by the\n   agreed harness.\" Name who runs the test, how long the customer has to accept or reject, and what\n   happens if they do not respond, for example deemed acceptance after 10 business days.\n5. **Customer responsibilities.** List each as a dated obligation: data extract in a specified format\n   by a date, SSO and network access by a date, SME hours per week for labelling and review, security\n   review turnaround. Then state the consequence when one slips: the timeline moves day for day.\n6. **Assumptions.** These are the facts the plan rests on, such as volumes, document formats, API\n   availability and model provider access. Each one, if false, triggers change control.\n7. **Timeline and milestones**, ideally phase-gated: discovery, then proof of concept, then pilot,\n   then production. Each gate has exit criteria and a go/no-go decision.\n8. **Change control.** How a change is requested, estimated, approved and priced. Without it, small\n   additions accumulate unpaid.\n9. **Roles, governance and support boundaries.** Who decides, the steering cadence, the escalation\n   path, and who operates, monitors and pays for inference after go-live.\n10. **Commercials.** Fixed fee, time and materials, or milestone-based payment, tied to the gates.\n\n### AI-specific clauses\n- **Eval set ownership and freeze.** Agree who builds the acceptance set (ideally jointly with the\n  customer's SMEs), that it is representative, and that it is frozen before tuning, so acceptance does\n  not chase a moving target.\n- **Model and provider changes.** State what happens if the provider deprecates the model or changes\n  pricing mid-project, and that re-validation counts as a change.\n- **Phase-based commitment.** Commit to quality targets only after a discovery or PoC phase has\n  measured feasibility on real data. Before that, commit to effort and learning, not outcomes.\n- **Sampling noise in thresholds.** A rate measured on N cases has an error bar, so say whether the\n  threshold applies to the point estimate or to a confidence bound, and size N to match. Zero-tolerance\n  lines need the same care. Zero failures in N cases shows, at 95% confidence, only that the true rate\n  is below about 3/N (the \"rule of three\"). If a failure type truly must never happen, the clause should\n  also require a deterministic control, such as a validator or a hard filter, not just a clean eval run.\n- **Post-acceptance performance.** Acceptance certifies behaviour on a frozen set at one date, and\n  production inputs drift afterwards. State the warranty period, who monitors which metrics after\n  go-live, the threshold that triggers remediation, and whether remediation is in scope or a change.\n- **Data handling.** State where customer data goes, retention, use (or no use) for training, and\n  deletion at the end. This often references the data processing agreement.","example":"Here is a worked rewrite of a weak SOW clause for an invoice-extraction pilot.\n\n```text\nWEAK\n  4.1 Vendor will deliver an AI system that accurately extracts invoice data.\n  5.1 Customer will provide sample invoices.\n\nSTRONG\n  4.1 Deliverable D2: extraction service deployed in Customer's staging tenant, writing to the\n      AP staging table defined in Appendix B.\n  4.2 Acceptance of D2: on the frozen acceptance set (600 invoices, stratified by the 12\n      highest-volume suppliers plus 100 long-tail invoices, labelled per Appendix C), measured\n      with the harness in Appendix D:\n        - invoice total, currency, supplier ID: >= 98% exact match each\n        - line items: >= 90% F1 at line level\n        - zero cases of a total written with the wrong currency\n      Customer has 10 business days to accept or list failing cases; silence = acceptance.\n  5.1 Customer provides 2,000 historical invoices with posted AP records (PDF + CSV export)\n      by 3 March; SSO access to staging by 10 March; 4 SME hours/week from the AP team.\n  5.2 Each business day of delay in 5.1 moves milestones M2-M4 by one business day.\n  6.3 Out of scope: handwritten invoices, non-Latin scripts, posting to the ERP ledger.\n```\n\nAll thresholds and dates here are illustrative. Notice what the strong version achieves. Acceptance\nis a computation anyone can re-run, not an opinion. The sample is stratified so that long-tail\nsuppliers, where extraction usually breaks, cannot be quietly excluded. The one catastrophic error\ntype gets its own zero-tolerance line, separate from the averages. The numbers are also honest about\ntheir resolution. On 600 invoices, 98% allows at most 12 misses per field, and a clean run on the\ncurrency line bounds the true wrong-currency rate below about 0.5% (3/600), not at zero. So the team\nalso builds a deterministic check that the extracted currency is one the vendor master allows for\nthat supplier, and that check is what makes \"zero\" credible. And the data dependency has a date\nand a consequence, so a late extract moves the schedule instead of eating the team's time. In week 4\nthe customer asks for posting to the ERP. Clause 6.3 makes that a change request with its own estimate\nrather than an argument.","tradeoffs":["Fixed-price SOWs give the customer budget certainty but make the vendor price research risk. Use them only after a PoC has measured feasibility on the customer's own data.","Time-and-materials terms absorb uncertainty well but give the customer little cost control. They suit discovery and PoC phases, and should give way to milestones once the target is known.","Tight, statistical acceptance criteria prevent disputes but take effort to agree on and to build the eval set for. That effort is worth it whenever payment or go-live depends on acceptance.","A long out-of-scope list protects the delivery team but can read as defensive and slow the signature. Focus it on the extensions customers most often assume are included.","Phase gates let both sides stop early, which caps loss. The cost is negotiating several times, which only pays off when uncertainty is high."],"pitfalls":["Acceptance criteria in prose such as \"accurate\" or \"production-ready\". The symptom is an acceptance meeting where the customer shows three wrong answers and refuses sign-off.","Customer dependencies without dates or consequences. The symptom is the team waiting weeks for data or access while the delivery date stays fixed.","Committing to a quality target before measuring feasibility on real data. The symptom is a project that is technically sound but contractually failed because the data was worse than the sample.","No support boundary after go-live. The symptom is the delivery team paged for production incidents months after the engagement ended.","An eval set the customer can change during the project. The symptom is acceptance thresholds being met and then the set being \"updated\" with new hard cases before sign-off."],"when":"Write an SOW, or its internal equivalent, for any engagement where someone outside the team must\naccept the result, and especially when money or a go-live date depends on it. Draft it at the end of\nscoping, once the success metrics, baseline plan and dependencies are known. Ideally, phase it so the\nfirst SOW covers only discovery and a PoC. For a two-week internal spike, a one-page brief with\nobjective, out of scope and an exit question is enough. In day-to-day work the SOW is the reference in\nevery scope discussion. In interviews and customer conversations, expect \"how would you write acceptance\ncriteria for a model?\" The answer is a frozen, representative eval set, per-slice thresholds, a\nzero-tolerance list and a named harness.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns acceptance clauses into a runnable eval harness and frozen dataset, so acceptance is a reproducible computation rather than a review meeting.","architect":"Sets the deliverable boundaries, environments, support model and the technical assumptions whose failure would trigger change control.","fde":"Drafts and negotiates the SOW with the customer, makes data and access dependencies dated obligations, and enforces change control on site."},"check":{"q":"A customer insists on a fixed-price SOW guaranteeing \"95% accuracy\" for a document-classification\nassistant, before you have seen their documents. How do you respond?","a":"I would not sign a quality guarantee for unseen data, because nobody knows whether 95% is\nfeasible, what \"accuracy\" covers, or what the human baseline is. I would propose a phased structure.\nFirst, a short fixed-fee discovery and PoC phase: we receive a representative sample, build a jointly\nlabelled eval set, measure the human baseline, and report achievable accuracy per category. Second, a\npilot or production SOW with acceptance criteria set from those measurements: per-class thresholds on\nthe frozen set, zero-tolerance error types, and dated customer obligations. This gives the customer an\nearly exit and a defensible target, and it stops either side from pricing a guess."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are acceptance criteria for model behaviour written against a frozen eval set?","options":["Frozen datasets run faster in the evaluation harness","A fixed, agreed set makes acceptance a reproducible computation and stops the target moving during tuning","Regulators require every model to be tested on frozen data","It lets the team tune the model directly on the acceptance cases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Probabilistic systems will always fail on some inputs, so acceptance must be a rate on a defined sample. Freezing the set prevents both sides from changing the test mid-project. Tuning on it would make the result meaningless."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of the customer-responsibilities section with dates and consequences?","options":["To shift all project risk onto the customer","To satisfy the MSA's liability terms","To list the customer's internal stakeholders","To make data, access and SME-time dependencies explicit so their delays move the schedule rather than silently consuming it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"AI delivery depends on customer data, access and expert time. Dated obligations with a stated consequence make slips visible and fair, rather than turning them into the delivery team's missed milestones."},{"q":"When does a fixed-price commitment to a quality target become reasonable for an AI project?","options":["When the vendor has hit that target with other customers","When the model provider publishes benchmark results above the target","After a discovery or PoC phase has measured feasibility and the baseline on the customer's own data","Never; AI projects should always be time-and-materials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality depends on the customer's data and task definition, so other customers' results or public benchmarks do not transfer. Once feasibility is measured on real data, the risk is known well enough to price."}]},{"key":"scoping/requirements-traceability","sources":["Gotel, Finkelstein, 1994 — An Analysis of the Requirements Traceability Problem"],"why":"Halfway through an AI project, the requirements live in three places: the SOW, a few Slack threads,\nand the heads of two SMEs. The eval suite has 800 cases, but nobody can say which requirement each\ncase checks. When the customer asks \"does it meet requirement 7, never discussing individual claims?\",\nthe honest answer is \"probably\". When someone switches to a cheaper model, the eval average moves\nfrom 91% to 90%, everyone shrugs, and nobody notices that every refusal case for requirement 7 now\nfails. Those were eight cases, about 1% of the suite, so their collapse moved the average by a single\npoint.\n\nThe naive approach treats the eval suite as one undifferentiated score, plus a requirements document\nthat is never updated. That breaks in both directions. Going forward, from requirements to tests,\nthere are requirements with no test at all, especially non-functional ones like \"never reveal another\nemployee's data\". Going backward, from tests and features to requirements, there are cases and\nfeatures that trace to nothing. Those are gold-plating, stale behaviour, or scope creep nobody approved.\nAggregate scores hide both. Regulated buyers make this concrete: in safety-critical software domains,\ntraceability from requirement to verification evidence is an expected part of certification. AI\ngovernance reviews increasingly ask the same question of model-based systems.","how":"### The chain\nGive every element a stable ID and record links between adjacent levels:\n\n1. **Objective** (from the metric tree), for example O1: reduce benefits-desk contacts.\n2. **Requirement**: a testable statement with a priority and an acceptance threshold. Include\n   functional requirements (answer from the current policy), behavioural ones (escalate medical-claim\n   questions), and non-functional ones (p95 latency, data isolation, retention).\n3. **Implementation elements**: the components, prompt templates, tools, retrieval filters and\n   guardrails that realise each requirement, recorded with their version.\n4. **Verification**: eval cases tagged with the requirement IDs they check, plus deterministic tests,\n   load tests and security tests for the non-functional requirements.\n5. **Evidence**: the run ID, the model and prompt versions, the date, and the per-requirement result.\n\n### Why per-requirement thresholds\nEach requirement gets its own acceptance threshold on its own slice of cases. The slice score, not\nthe global average, decides the result *because* requirements differ in criticality and in volume. A\n100% requirement such as \"never reveal another employee's data\" can be outvoted by hundreds of\nroutine cases in an average. Slicing also gives each requirement a sample size, so you can tell\nwhether a slice has enough cases for its result to mean anything.\n\nSample size matters most for zero-tolerance requirements. If a slice of n cases has no failures, the\n95% upper bound on the true failure rate is about 3/n (the \"rule of three\"). Thirty clean cases show\nonly that the rate is below roughly 10%; three hundred show below roughly 1%. A 100% threshold on a\nsmall slice is therefore evidence of \"not frequent\", not of \"never\". A requirement that truly must\nnever fail should also trace to a deterministic control, such as a permission filter or a hard\nvalidator with its own unit tests, with the eval slice as a second line of evidence.\n\n### The two queries that matter\n- **Coverage (forward):** which requirements have no verification, or too few cases to judge? Fill\n  these gaps before acceptance, not after an incident.\n- **Orphans (backward):** which tests, prompts or features trace to no requirement? Either a\n  requirement is missing from the document, and should be added and approved, or the element is\n  unneeded and should be removed.\n\n### Change impact analysis\nWhen something changes, the links say what to re-verify. A new model version touches every\nrequirement, so re-run everything and compare per-requirement results. A change to the retrieval\nfilter touches the requirements linked to that component. A requirement change touches its linked\ncases, which may need relabelling. This is the payoff that justifies the bookkeeping: the cost of\nchange becomes proportional to what changed, rather than \"re-test everything and hope\".\n\n### Keep it lightweight\nThe matrix does not need a requirements-management tool. Tags on eval cases (a `reqs` field in the\nJSONL), a requirements file in the repo with IDs and thresholds, and a CI step that prints the\nper-requirement report cover most AI projects. A traceability matrix that is not generated from the\nartefacts themselves goes stale within weeks.","example":"An HR benefits assistant. Eval cases carry requirement tags, and CI prints a per-requirement report\nthat catches untested requirements and orphan cases. A real suite would have tens to hundreds of cases\nper requirement. This one is cut down to show the mechanics.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nREQS = {   # from the SOW: id -> (statement, acceptance threshold on its slice)\n    \"R1\": (\"Answer benefits questions from the current policy only\", 0.95),\n    \"R2\": (\"Refuse and escalate questions about individual medical claims\", 1.00),\n    \"R3\": (\"Cite the policy section for every factual answer\", 0.90),\n    \"R4\": (\"Never reveal another employee's data\", 1.00),\n}\nCASES = [  # eval cases tagged with the requirements they verify, plus latest result\n    {\"id\": \"c001\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\", \"R3\"], \"passed\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"c002\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\"],       \"passed\": True},\n    {\"id\": \"c003\", \"reqs\": [\"R2\"],       \"passed\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"c004\", \"reqs\": [\"R1\", \"R3\"], \"passed\": False},\n    {\"id\": \"c005\", \"reqs\": [\"R9\"],       \"passed\": True},   # tag points at no requirement\n    {\"id\": \"c006\", \"reqs\": [],           \"passed\": True},   # untagged case\n]\nMIN_CASES = 30   # below this, a slice result is too noisy to accept on\n\nby_req, orphans = defaultdict(list), []\nfor c in CASES:\n    known = [r for r in c[\"reqs\"] if r in REQS]\n    if not known:\n        orphans.append(c[\"id\"])\n    for r in known:\n        by_req[r].append(c[\"passed\"])\n\nfor rid, (text, threshold) in REQS.items():\n    res = by_req.get(rid, [])\n    if not res:\n        print(f\"{rid} UNTESTED  {text}\")\n        continue\n    rate = sum(res) / len(res)\n    note = \"  (too few cases)\" if len(res) < MIN_CASES else \"\"\n    print(f\"{rid} {'PASS' if rate >= threshold else 'FAIL'}  {rate:.0%} of {len(res)} \"\n          f\"(needs {threshold:.0%}){note}  {text}\")\nprint(\"orphans:\", orphans)   # -> ['c005', 'c006']\n```\n\nThe output shows R4, the data-isolation requirement, as UNTESTED. That would never show up in an\naggregate pass rate, and it is exactly the requirement a security reviewer will ask about. R2 fails\noutright, and its 100% threshold means a single failure blocks acceptance. Both orphans need a\ndecision: is c005's \"R9\" a typo, or a requirement nobody wrote down? And when R4 does get cases, a\n30-case slice passing at 100% only bounds the leak rate below about 10%. That is why R4 should also\ntrace to the retrieval layer's per-employee access filter and its deterministic tests, not to the\neval slice alone.","tradeoffs":["Fine-grained tagging gives precise impact analysis but costs time on every new case. Tag at the requirement level, not the sub-clause level, unless the domain is regulated.","A dedicated requirements tool gives audit trails and approval workflows but drifts away from the code. Prefer IDs in repo files and eval data unless the customer's compliance process mandates a tool.","Per-requirement thresholds are stricter than a global average and can block releases over small slices. Pair them with minimum case counts so noise does not decide acceptance.","Tracing every prompt template to requirements makes refactoring slower. It earns its cost once the system has more than a handful of prompts or more than one team editing them."],"pitfalls":["Reporting a single aggregate eval score as proof of acceptance. The symptom is a release that passes on average while a small, critical requirement fails every case.","Non-functional requirements left out of the matrix. The symptom is a security review finding that data isolation or retention was never tested at all.","A hand-maintained traceability spreadsheet. The symptom is a matrix that lists cases which no longer exist and misses the ones added last sprint.","Not recording model and prompt versions with the evidence. The symptom is nobody being able to say which configuration actually passed acceptance once something regresses in production.","Treating orphan cases as harmless. The symptom is behaviour customers rely on that is undocumented, untested against a threshold, and removed by the next refactor."],"when":"Set up traceability as soon as there is an SOW with numbered requirements and more than a few dozen\neval cases. In practice that means at the start of the pilot build, because that is when tagging is\ncheapest. It is essential when acceptance is contractual, when several teams change prompts or models,\nor when the buyer is regulated and will audit. For a throwaway PoC, a short list of requirements\nmapped to test names is enough. Day to day, it shows up as a per-requirement CI report and as the\nfirst thing you check when a model upgrade is proposed. In interviews, it answers \"how do you know a\nmodel swap didn't break anything?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tags eval cases with requirement IDs, writes the per-requirement CI report, and records model and prompt versions with every run's evidence.","architect":"Defines the ID scheme and the links between requirements, components and evals, so change impact analysis works across teams and releases.","fde":"Keeps the customer's requirements and the matrix aligned on site, and uses the report as acceptance evidence with the customer's QA or compliance team."},"check":{"q":"The team wants to switch to a cheaper model. The overall eval pass rate drops from 92% to 91%.\nWhy is that not enough to approve the change, and what would you look at?","a":"An aggregate hides how the drop is distributed. One point overall could be noise spread evenly,\nor it could be every case of a small, critical requirement failing, such as refusals for regulated\nquestions or data isolation. I would run the per-requirement report from the traceability matrix.\nThat means checking each requirement's slice against its own threshold, flagging slices with too few\ncases to judge, and diffing per-requirement results between the two models. A model change touches\nevery requirement, so all of them need to be re-verified. I would approve only if every critical\nrequirement still meets its threshold, and record the new model version with the evidence."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a backward trace (from tests and features to requirements) mainly reveal?","options":["Requirements that have no test coverage","Tests, prompts or features that serve no agreed requirement, such as gold-plating or undocumented scope","The model version that produced each result","Which requirements have the highest business value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forward tracing finds untested requirements. Backward tracing finds elements that trace to nothing. Those indicate missing requirements to approve, or unneeded work and unapproved scope to remove."},{"q":"Why should each requirement be accepted on its own slice and threshold rather than the global eval score?","options":["Slices run faster in CI than the full suite","Global scores are not statistically valid","It lets the team drop hard cases from the average","A small but critical requirement can fail completely while the average barely moves"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Requirements differ in criticality and case count. A per-requirement threshold means a zero-tolerance behaviour such as data isolation decides acceptance on its own, rather than being outvoted by many routine cases."},{"q":"Which change requires re-verifying every requirement, not just a subset?","options":["Editing the citation format prompt for one answer type","Adding ten new eval cases to the escalation slice","Upgrading the underlying model to a new version","Renaming a requirement ID in the requirements file"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model affects every behaviour, so impact analysis links it to all requirements. A prompt edit touches only the requirements linked to that template, and adding cases or renaming IDs changes verification or bookkeeping, not behaviour."}]},{"key":"scoping/dependency-and-risk-management","sources":["Official docs: NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0)"],"why":"A typical enterprise AI pilot plan says \"weeks 1–2: data ingestion\". In practice, week 1 is spent\nlearning that the document system's export needs a ticket to a team with a three-week queue. Week 3\nreveals that a third of the documents are scanned images. Week 5 brings the security review's verdict\nthat customer data cannot go to an external API in that region. None of these is an engineering\nfailure, yet each one moves the delivery date, and the team takes the blame because nobody wrote the\ndependency down with an owner and a date.\n\nThe naive approaches fail predictably. A risk list written once for the kickoff deck and never\nreopened gives no protection: it records risks and drives no action. Rating everything \"medium\" makes\nit impossible to prioritise. Treating risks as things to watch rather than things to retire leaves the\nbiggest unknowns, such as data quality, security approval and feasibility on real inputs, unresolved\nuntil late, when there is no slack left to absorb them. AI work also carries risks ordinary software\nprojects do not have. The data may not support the task. The eval set may not represent production.\nModel behaviour can change when a provider updates or retires a version. Quotas and regional\navailability can block a deployment design that worked in a demo.","how":"### Keep a RAID log\nKeep one living log of **R**isks (things that might happen), **A**ssumptions (things you are treating\nas true but have not verified), **I**ssues (risks that have already happened) and **D**ependencies\n(inputs owned by someone else). The categories matter *because* each one triggers a different action.\nAn assumption gets verified. A risk gets a response. An issue gets resolved now. A dependency gets\nchased by its owner.\n\n### Map dependencies\nFor each dependency, record what is needed (in a specific format), who owns it (a named person, not\n\"IT\"), the realistic lead time, the need-by date derived from the plan, and the status. Then find the\n**critical path**: the chain of dependent tasks with zero slack, such as data export → eval set →\nPoC → pilot sign-off. Anything on that chain slips the end date day for day. Anything off it has slack\nyou can spend. Start the longest-lead items first. Security reviews, legal agreements and procurement\noften take longer than the engineering, so they should start in week zero, before anything is built.\n\n### Identify risks by category\nWalk a checklist so you do not rely on memory:\n- **Data:** availability, quality, volume, representativeness, rights to use.\n- **Access and security:** credentials, network paths, approval of external model APIs, data residency.\n- **Technical and model:** feasibility on real inputs, latency or cost at volume, provider quotas,\n  deprecation of a model version.\n- **People and adoption:** SME availability, sponsor turnover, user resistance.\n- **Commercial and vendor:** pricing changes, contract terms, lock-in.\n- **Regulatory and harm:** whether outputs could harm users or breach rules. This overlaps with\n  AI-system risk management (frameworks such as the NIST AI RMF), which is covered in its own lesson.\n\n### Assess in units people can act on\nScore each risk by likelihood and impact. Where possible, express impact in schedule days or money,\nnot \"high\". The expected delay, probability × impact, can be compared directly with the slack on the\naffected path. A 50% chance of a 15-day slip on the critical path is 7.5 expected days, which outranks\na 90% chance of a 2-day slip on a path with 10 days of slack.\n\nExpected delay ranks risks, but it is not the whole story. A 10% chance of a 60-day slip has the same\n6 expected days as a near-certain 6-day slip, yet only the first can end the engagement. For risks\nwhose impact would be fatal, such as the pilot being unable to run at all or a budget window closing,\nplan by impact rather than by expected value: buy them down early or prepare a contingency whatever\ntheir expected days say.\n\n### Choose a response and a trigger\nFor each significant risk, choose one of four responses. **Avoid** it by changing the plan. **Reduce**\nit with a spike, a fallback or an earlier start. **Transfer** it to the party best placed to own it,\nfor example by making a data date a customer obligation in the SOW. Or **accept** it with a\ncontingency plan. Give each risk an early-warning trigger, such as \"export not received by day 10\".\nThat way, switching to the contingency is a pre-agreed step rather than a debate held in a crisis.\n\n### Retire the biggest unknowns first\nOrder early work by the uncertainty it removes, not by the order the architecture suggests. A two-day\nspike on 50 real documents removes more risk than two weeks of polished pipeline code over synthetic\nones. Review the log weekly at the steering meeting, with escalation thresholds agreed in the SOW.","example":"A 10-week pilot of a contract-review assistant for a legal department. All figures are illustrative.\nAt kickoff the RAID log holds these top items:\n\n| ID | Item | Owner | Prob | Impact | Exp. days | Response and trigger |\n|----|------|-------|------|--------|-----------|----------------------|\n| D1 | DMS export of 3,000 contracts | Customer IT | — | critical path | — | Ticket raised day 0; need-by end of week 2 |\n| R1 | Security review rejects external LLM API | Customer CISO | 0.3 | 20 days | 6 | Start review day 0; adapter layer allows in-region hosted model |\n| R2 | Many contracts are scans with poor OCR | Delivery lead | 0.5 | 10 days | 5 | Spike on 50 real files in week 1 |\n| R3 | SME lawyers miss labelling sessions | Customer GC | 0.6 | 5 days | 3 | Book sessions now; trigger if <75% attended in week 3 |\n| R4 | Provider quota too low for batch backfill | Engineer | 0.2 | 3 days | 0.6 | Request increase week 1; accept |\n\nD1, then R1 and R2, sit on the critical path, because the eval set needs the export and the pilot\nneeds security approval. Plan slack is 5 days. The expected delays from R1 and R2 alone (11 days)\nexceed it, so the plan as drawn is not credible. The team acts accordingly. It submits the security\nquestionnaire on day 0, not when the pilot is ready. It runs the OCR spike in week 1. The spike finds\nthat 30% of files are scans and that OCR is acceptable on 80% of them, so R2 drops to a 3-day impact.\nIt also writes D1's need-by date into the SOW, so that a late export moves the schedule. In week 4 the\nsecurity review asks for an in-region deployment. Because R1 had a contingency, the switch takes 4 days\ninstead of the 20 it would have cost unplanned.","tradeoffs":["A detailed RAID log catches more surprises but costs steering time every week. Keep the top ten items active and archive the rest, unless the engagement is regulated or multi-vendor.","Front-loading spikes removes uncertainty early but delays visible demo progress. That trade is worth making whenever a feasibility or data risk sits on the critical path.","Transferring risk to the customer through dated obligations protects the schedule but can strain the relationship. Frame it as shared planning, and only for items they genuinely control.","Building contingency architecture, such as a provider adapter or in-region fallback, costs effort upfront. It pays off when security or provider approval is uncertain, and is waste when approval is already confirmed.","Quantified scoring (probability × days) gives real prioritisation but implies false precision. Treat the numbers as ranking tools, not forecasts, when the estimates are rough."],"pitfalls":["Writing the risk list once at kickoff. The symptom is a deck slide that still lists \"data quality\" as medium in week 8, after it has already caused a slip.","Naming teams instead of people as owners. The symptom is a dependency marked \"with IT\" for three weeks with nobody able to say who is working on it.","Starting security and legal reviews only when the system is ready. The symptom is a finished pilot that waits a month for approval to touch production data.","Accepting risks without triggers or contingencies. The symptom is a crisis meeting debating options that could have been decided calmly in week one.","Ignoring model and provider risks. The symptom is a deployment design that fails on quota, regional availability or a deprecated model version in the final week."],"when":"Start the dependency map and RAID log during scoping, before the SOW is signed, because dated customer\ndependencies and known risks belong in the SOW itself. Then review the log weekly for the life of the\nengagement. Even a two-week internal PoC benefits from a ten-line version naming the data, access and\npeople it depends on. Measure how the log is working: count dependencies past their need-by date, compare the expected\ndelay with slack on the critical path, and track total exposure (the sum of expected days on the\ncritical path) week by week. Exposure should fall as spikes retire unknowns. A flat or rising curve\nmeans the work is not attacking the risks. In interviews and on site, expect \"what could\nstop this project?\" The strong answer names specific, owned dependencies and the spike that would\nretire the biggest unknown first.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the early spikes that retire technical and data risks, and builds the adapters and fallbacks that turn contingencies into small switches.","architect":"Identifies architectural risks such as data residency, provider lock-in, quotas and deprecation, and designs seams so the contingency is cheap to execute.","fde":"Owns the RAID log with the customer, chases named owners for access and data, and escalates through the steering group when a trigger fires."},"check":{"q":"You are two weeks into a 12-week pilot. The data export is late, SMEs have missed one labelling\nsession, and the security review has not started. How do you prioritise, and what do you change?","a":"First I would map which items sit on the critical path. The security review usually has the\nlongest lead time and gates any production-data pilot, so not having started it is the biggest risk.\nI would get it submitted this week, with an architecture fallback ready in case external APIs are\nrejected. The late export is a dependency on the critical path, so I would name a specific owner,\nagree a new date, and invoke the SOW clause that moves milestones day for day. The missed SME session\nis a risk to watch: rebook it and set an attendance trigger. Then I would re-estimate expected delay\nagainst remaining slack and tell the sponsor now whether the end date still holds."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a security review or data-processing agreement usually start in week zero?","options":["Reviewers prefer to see an early design rather than a finished system","It is a legal requirement before any code is written","It often has the longest lead time and gates the pilot, so starting late puts its full duration on the critical path","Starting early lets the team skip the review if nobody objects"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Approval processes are frequently slower than the engineering and block access to real data. Starting them first runs them in parallel with building, instead of serially after it."},{"q":"A risk has a 50% chance of a 15-day slip on the critical path; another has a 90% chance of a 2-day slip on a path with 10 days of slack. Which matters more?","options":["The second, because it is more likely to happen","The first, because its expected delay of 7.5 days lands directly on the end date","They are equal, because both are rated likely","Neither, because risks under 20 days are acceptable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expected delay is probability × impact, and a slip moves the end date only by the amount it exceeds the slack on its path. The second risk's 2-day slip, even if it happens, is fully absorbed by 10 days of slack, while the first lands directly on the end date with 7.5 expected days."},{"q":"What distinguishes an assumption from a risk in a RAID log?","options":["Assumptions are low-impact risks","Risks are owned by the customer, while assumptions are owned by the vendor","Assumptions are issues that have already happened","An assumption is something treated as true but unverified, so its action is to verify it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each RAID category has a different action. Assumptions get verified, because a false one usually becomes a risk or issue. Risks get a response and a trigger, issues get resolved now, and dependencies get chased with their owner."}]},{"key":"scoping/timeboxing","sources":[],"why":"Classic software estimation assumes the work is mostly known: you can list the features and the effort is\nroughly the sum of the parts. AI work breaks that assumption. Whether an LLM can extract fields from this\ncustomer's scanned invoices at 95% accuracy is an empirical question nobody can answer by thinking; you\nfind out by trying. And the trying has no natural end. Accuracy climbs fast at first, then flattens: the\nfirst day of prompt work might move a metric from 60% to 80%, the next week from 80% to 85%, and the\nfollowing month from 85% to 87%. There is always one more prompt variant, one more retrieval tweak, one\nmore model to try.\n\nThe naive alternative is to estimate the work (\"the extraction feature will take three weeks\") and track\nagainst it. That fails because the estimate is a guess about an unknown, and when it is wrong the\nteam keeps going, since stopping feels like admitting failure and the next tweak always looks promising.\nThe result is the familiar pattern of a proof of concept that is \"almost there\" for three months.\n\nA timebox inverts the commitment. Instead of promising an outcome by a date, you promise a *decision* by a\ndate, backed by evidence. Scope is the variable: if the box closes and the target is not met, that is\ninformation, not a slip. This also protects the customer relationship, because the customer is buying\na de-risked answer on a known schedule rather than an open-ended research project.","how":"### 1. Name the question, not the task\nA good timebox answers one question with a yes/no or a number: \"Can we reach 90% field-level accuracy on\ntheir real invoice sample with an off-the-shelf model?\" A bad one names an activity: \"Work on extraction.\"\nThe question form matters *because* it tells you when you are done and what evidence counts.\n\n### 2. Set the budget from the value of the answer\nThe length of the box should match what the answer is worth and how much uncertainty it removes, not how\nlong the work \"should\" take. A question that gates a large contract justifies two weeks; a question about\nwhich of two chunking strategies to use justifies a day. A rough value-of-information test keeps this\nhonest:\n\n```\nworth boxing if   cost of box  <  P(answer changes the decision) × cost of deciding wrongly\n```\n\nThree engineers for two weeks is about 30 person-days. If the answer has a one-in-three chance of\nflipping a decision that would otherwise sink six months of a larger team into the wrong design, the box\nis cheap. If the team would make the same choice whatever the result, the box buys nothing and should\nbe skipped or shrunk. Typical boxes are hours (a spike), days (a feasibility check) or a few weeks (a\nPoC), and almost never open-ended. Scrum sprints are timeboxes too, but for delivery cadence; the\ndiscovery-style box here differs in that \"stop\" is an expected, legitimate outcome.\n\n### 3. Write the exit criteria before starting\nBefore any work, write down the decision rule: what result means go, what means stop, and what means\npivot. For example: \"≥ 90% on the 100-document set: proceed to pilot. 75–90%: one more week focused only\non the top two error categories. Below 75%: stop and recommend a human-in-the-loop design.\" Pre-committing\nmatters *because* after a week of effort everyone is anchored on their sunk cost, and criteria written\nafterwards drift toward whatever was achieved.\n\n### 4. Order the work by risk, with a scope ladder\nSplit scope into must, should and could. Do the riskiest must-have first, *because* if it fails nothing\nelse matters and you want to learn that on day two, not day nine. Build the evaluation set before tuning,\nso every hour of tuning moves a number you trust.\n\n### 5. Checkpoint at fixed points\nA two-week box gets a midpoint check: are we on a trajectory to the target, or flat? Make it arithmetic\nrather than mood:\n\n```\nrequired rate = (target − current) / days remaining\nobserved rate = gain over the last few working days / those days\n```\n\nIf the observed rate is well below the required rate, the box will not close the gap by grinding, and\nthe second week should cut scope, change approach, or prepare a stop recommendation. Two cautions apply.\nLearning curves bend down, so the observed rate overstates the future one. And a gain only counts if it\nexceeds the eval set's noise: on 100 documents, a one-point move can come from a handful of borderline\ncases flipping, so \"we went from 86% to 87%\" may be no movement at all.\n\n### 6. Close the box with a decision record\nAt the end, record the question, the evidence, the decision and what was learned, including dead ends.\nExtending a box is allowed, but only as an explicit new box with its own question and criteria, never as a\nsilent slide.","example":"A forward-deployed team gets two weeks (10 working days) to test whether an LLM can extract 12 fields from\na logistics customer's bills of lading.\n\n- **Question:** can we reach ≥ 90% field-level accuracy on the customer's real documents?\n- **Exit rule (written day 0):** ≥ 90% → pilot; 80–90% → one focused extra week; < 80% → recommend a\n  review-queue design where the model drafts and a clerk confirms.\n- **Days 1–2:** obtain 150 real documents, have the customer's clerks label 100 of them (1,200 field\n  values). This is the must-have; without it every later number is opinion.\n- **Day 3:** baseline with a strong general model and a simple prompt: 71% (852 of 1,200 fields correct,\n  348 wrong).\n- **Days 4–5:** error analysis shows about 40% of the 348 errors (roughly 140, out of only 300 values in\n  those fields) come from three fields: the consignee address on multi-line stamps, the weight unit and\n  the date format. Fixing those with explicit format instructions and examples: 83% (about 996 correct).\n- **Midpoint check (day 5):** at 83% with five days left, the required rate is (90 − 83) / 5 = 1.4 points\n  per day; the last two days delivered about 6 points per day. Even allowing for the curve flattening,\n  continuing is justified. Drop the \"could\" item (a review UI) to protect the time.\n- **Days 6–9:** OCR preprocessing for stamped regions moves accuracy to 88%; further prompt variants add\n  under a point.\n- **Day 10:** 88% (about 1,056 correct, 144 wrong) lands in the 80–90% band. Error analysis shows roughly\n  60% of the remaining errors sit in the ~20 documents with handwritten weights and annotations. Decision\n  recorded: one more week, scoped only to handwritten documents, with its own pass/fail threshold.\n\nThe numbers here are illustrative, but the shape is typical: rapid early gains, then a plateau that only a\npre-written rule stops the team from chasing indefinitely.","tradeoffs":["Fixed time gives predictable schedules and forces decisions, but you may stop just short of a working solution; this flips when the remaining gap is a known, well-scoped engineering problem.","Short boxes reduce sunk cost and expose dead ends early, but too-short boxes cannot produce a trustworthy eval set, so the decision rests on anecdotes instead of measurements.","Pre-written exit criteria resist motivated reasoning, but they can be wrong if written before you understood the data; allow one explicit revision at the first checkpoint, with the reason recorded.","Cutting scope keeps the date, but repeatedly cutting the same must-have item means the question itself was too large and should have been decomposed into smaller boxes."],"pitfalls":["Silently extending the box (\"just two more days\") until the PoC has run for months; the symptom is a status update that says \"almost there\" three times in a row.","Timeboxing an activity instead of a question, so the box ends with work done but no decision; the symptom is a closing meeting where nobody can say whether to proceed.","Tuning prompts before building the evaluation set, so progress is judged by eyeballing outputs; the symptom is accuracy claims that collapse when someone runs a held-out sample.","Setting the box length by how long the work feels like it should take rather than by the value of the answer, which shows up as two-week investigations into decisions worth a day.","Treating a \"stop\" outcome as a team failure, so nobody ever recommends stopping; the symptom is an organisation whose boxes all end in \"promising, continue\" and never in a stop."],"when":"Reach for a timebox whenever the outcome is uncertain and the work could expand without limit: feasibility\nchecks, PoCs, model or vendor bake-offs, debugging an unexplained quality drop, and any research spike\ninside a delivery project. Do not timebox well-understood engineering work, where ordinary estimation is\nfine and the scope is fixed by requirements. Measure progress by the metric the question names, plotted\nover the box, not by hours spent. In interviews and customer conversations, a timebox is how you say\n\"we will know by Friday\" credibly: you name the question, the evidence and the decision rule up front.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval set first, plots the metric daily, and flags a flat curve at the midpoint instead of quietly trying more prompt variants.","architect":"Decides which unknowns get boxed and in what order, so the riskiest architectural assumption is tested before the team invests in surrounding infrastructure.","fde":"Sells a fixed-length discovery or PoC engagement with written exit criteria, which keeps the customer relationship anchored on a decision date rather than an open-ended promise."},"check":{"q":"Your two-week PoC ends at 84% accuracy against a 90% target, the team is confident another week will\nget there, and the customer is enthusiastic. What do you do?","a":"Go back to the exit rule written at the start. If it defined a middle band, follow it; if not, treat\nan extension as a new box, not a continuation. Ask what the error analysis says: if most remaining errors\ncluster in one or two identifiable categories with a concrete fix, a one-week box scoped only to those is\njustified, with its own pass/fail threshold. If errors are diffuse and the curve has flattened, another\nweek of tuning is unlikely to close a six-point gap, so recommend a design that tolerates 84% (human review,\nnarrower scope) instead. Enthusiasm is not evidence; the trajectory and error distribution are."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the defining feature of a timebox compared with an ordinary estimate?","options":["It uses smaller units of time, such as hours instead of days","Time is fixed and scope flexes, with an explicit decision when the time runs out","Scope is fixed and the deadline moves when new information appears","It removes the need for success criteria because the date is the criterion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An estimate predicts how long fixed scope will take; a timebox fixes the time and ends in a decision. The date alone is not a criterion; you still need the question and exit rule to know what the result means."},{"q":"Why should exit criteria be written before the timeboxed work begins?","options":["So the team can ask the customer to sign them as part of the contract","Because criteria cannot be measured until the eval set exists","Because after investing effort, people anchor on sunk cost and criteria drift toward whatever was achieved","Because the model provider requires a target accuracy for evaluation access"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pre-commitment protects the decision from motivated reasoning. Contract signature can help but is not the reason; the core issue is that judgment after effort is biased toward continuing."},{"q":"At the midpoint of a two-week box, accuracy has been flat for three days. What is the best response?","options":["Keep going, since the plan allocated two weeks and the second week may bring gains","Immediately extend the box by a week to give the approach a fair chance","Treat it as an early signal: cut scope, change approach, or prepare a stop recommendation","Switch to measuring hours spent so the effort is visible to stakeholders"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checkpoints exist to detect a flat trajectory early. Continuing unchanged spends the second week hoping; extending without a new question is a silent slide, and hours spent measure effort, not learning."}]},{"key":"scoping/demos","sources":[],"why":"Most people who fund, approve or adopt an AI system will never read an eval report. They form their view\nfrom what they see in a meeting. That makes the demo one of the highest-leverage artifacts in an\nengagement, and also one of the most hazardous.\n\nThe hazard is specific to generative AI. With conventional software, if a feature works once on stage it\nmostly works every time, because behavior is deterministic and covered by tests. With an LLM, the same\nprompt can produce different outputs across runs, and quality varies enormously across inputs. It takes an\nafternoon to find five inputs where a prototype looks brilliant; it takes weeks to make the median input\ngood. A demo built on those five inputs creates expectations the system cannot meet, and the gap surfaces\nin the pilot as \"it worked in the demo\".\n\nThe arithmetic makes the problem concrete. Suppose the system is right on 76% of real inputs. Show ten\nhand-picked successes and the audience's implied estimate is close to 100%, a 24-point overstatement\nnobody said out loud. Pick five inputs genuinely at random instead, and the chance all five succeed is\n0.76⁵ ≈ 0.25, so three demos in four would show at least one failure. Neither extreme is acceptable:\nthe first misleads, the second leaves the story to chance. The demo has to be *designed*.\n\nThe naive alternatives both fail. The polished-showcase demo (curated inputs, rehearsed path, everything\ngreen) wins the meeting and loses the account later. The opposite, refusing to demo until the system is\n\"ready\", leaves stakeholders without anything concrete to react to, so feedback on the workflow arrives\nmonths late, when changes are expensive. The useful middle is a demo designed around a decision, built on\nrepresentative inputs, honest about its boundaries, and backed by numbers.","how":"### 1. Start from the decision and the audience\nBefore building anything, write one sentence: \"After this demo, *who* should be able to decide *what*?\"\nAn executive deciding whether to fund a pilot needs the business outcome and the risk. The operations\nlead deciding whether her team will use it needs to see her own workflow, including the awkward cases.\nThe security reviewer needs the data flow. One demo rarely serves all three, *because* each decision\nneeds different evidence.\n\n### 2. Script around a real workflow with real data\nUse the customer's own documents, tickets or records (with permission and appropriate handling), arranged\nas the task a real user performs from start to finish. Real data matters *because* it carries the mess\nsynthetic data lacks: scanned pages, abbreviations, missing fields. Recognizable inputs also make the\naudience trust what they see.\n\n### 3. Choose inputs representatively, and say how\nPick demo inputs from the evaluation set, roughly in proportion to its error categories: some typical\ncases, one hard case, and one case the system gets wrong or escalates. Selection is fine; *undisclosed*\nselection is the problem. Then state the aggregate: \"On 200 of your past tickets, it routed 87% correctly; here\nare three of them.\" The demo is the anecdote, the eval is the evidence, and showing both prevents the\nanecdote from standing in for the evidence.\n\n### 4. Show the boundary on purpose\nDemonstrate what happens when the system is unsure: it abstains, cites its sources, or hands off to a\nhuman. Showing a controlled failure builds more trust than a flawless run, *because* experienced buyers\nknow nothing is flawless, and they want to see that failures are contained.\n\n### 5. Control the live risks\nLive demos of non-deterministic systems fail in ways rehearsals miss: provider latency spikes, rate\nlimits, a different output on the same input. Run-to-run variance compounds across segments: if each of\nfour live inputs gives the intended result on 9 of 10 rehearsal runs, the chance at least one goes wrong\non the day is 1 − 0.9⁴ ≈ 34%. So rehearse each live input many times and measure its stability; inputs\nthat flip between runs belong in the recorded or narrated part, or should be shown deliberately as a\nvariance example. Mitigations: lower sampling temperature for the demo path\nwhere the model exposes it (this reduces variance but does not guarantee identical outputs, and some\nreasoning models do not accept a temperature setting), pre-warm caches, have a recorded backup of each segment, and test on the actual network you will present\nfrom. If you show cached or recorded output, say so, *because* hidden caching also hides real latency.\n\n### 6. Label what is real\nSeparate what is built from what is mocked: \"The extraction is live; the CRM write-back is a stub.\" Any\nUI, integration or data source that is faked becomes an implied commitment unless it is labeled.\n\n### 7. Capture the reaction as data\nAssign someone to record questions, objections and feature requests. These are discovery inputs and often\nreveal the real success criterion, such as \"the summary is nice, but what I need is the deadline date\".","example":"A 25-minute demo of a contract-review assistant to a legal operations team deciding whether to run a\nsix-week pilot.\n\n| Minutes | Segment | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0–3 | The decision on the table and today's process: 40 minutes per NDA review, two reviewers | Anchor on their baseline |\n| 3–10 | Live run on three of *their* past NDAs: two typical, one with an unusual indemnity clause | Show the real workflow |\n| 10–14 | Results on 120 historical NDAs: clause-flag recall 91%, precision 84%, misses listed by type | Evidence behind the anecdote |\n| 14–18 | A document where the model is unsure: it flags low confidence and routes to a lawyer | Show the boundary |\n| 18–21 | What is live vs. stubbed (the document-management integration is mocked) | Prevent implied commitments |\n| 21–25 | Proposed pilot scope, success metric and their questions | Turn reaction into a decision |\n\nThe figures are illustrative. Note what is absent: no curated \"wow\" prompt, no unexplained accuracy\nclaim, and no integration shown as working when it is not. The live segment has a recorded fallback, and\nthe session ends on the decision it was built to inform.","tradeoffs":["Live demos build credibility because the audience sees real behavior, but they risk visible failure; recorded segments are safer when the network or provider is unreliable, provided you say they are recorded.","Showing failures honestly costs some initial excitement, but it pays back in the pilot; the calculation flips only in pure exploratory sessions where nobody is making a commitment.","Early, rough demos surface workflow feedback while it is cheap to act on, but they risk anchoring stakeholders on unfinished UI; label rough parts explicitly to keep feedback focused.","Tailoring a demo to one audience makes it persuasive for that decision, but reusing it for a different audience usually answers the wrong question and should prompt a rebuild."],"pitfalls":["Demoing on hand-picked inputs that are easier than real traffic; the symptom is a pilot whose users say the system \"worked better in the demo\".","Showing mocked integrations or UI without labeling them, which turns them into commitments; the symptom is a statement of work that includes features nobody estimated.","Demoing on synthetic or public data instead of the customer's own, so the audience cannot judge relevance and the hard cases never appear; the symptom is polite approval and no follow-up.","Rehearsing only on a fast office network, then presenting through a customer VPN or proxy; the symptom is timeouts or long spinners during the live segment.","Treating the demo as the evaluation, with no aggregate number behind it; the symptom is stakeholders quoting a single impressive example as the expected performance.","Special-casing the demo inputs in code (a hard-coded lookup, a prompt tuned to those three documents); the symptom is the same inputs behaving differently once the pilot build replaces the demo branch.","Letting the demo screens become the specification by default; the symptom is change requests and SOW lines that cite \"as shown in the demo\" for behavior nobody designed or estimated."],"when":"Demo at decision points: after discovery to confirm you understood the workflow, at the end of a PoC to\ndecide on a pilot, during a pilot to show progress against the metric, and before production for sign-off.\nDemo early and roughly when the main risk is building the wrong thing; demo later and with evidence when\nthe main risk is overpromising. Do not use a demo to substitute for an evaluation, and do not demo to an\naudience whose decision you have not identified. Measure a demo by whether it produced a clear decision\nand actionable feedback, not by applause.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds a reproducible demo path from the eval set, with fixed settings, warmed caches and a recorded fallback, and makes sure the aggregate numbers shown are real.","architect":"Decides what the demo claims about the target architecture and labels which components are prototype stand-ins, so the demo does not quietly redefine the design.","fde":"Runs demos inside the customer's environment on their data, reads the room for the real success criterion, and turns objections into scoped pilot requirements."},"check":{"q":"A sales lead asks you to demo a support-ticket triage prototype to the customer's VP next week, using\nthe ten examples that \"look best\". The prototype's accuracy on a 300-ticket eval set is 76%. How do you\nbuild the demo?","a":"First pin down the VP's decision, probably whether to fund a pilot. Then pick demo tickets from the\neval set to reflect the real mix: a few typical correct cases, one hard case, and one wrong or escalated\ncase showing how errors are caught. Present the 76% alongside the current baseline and what the pilot would\naim to improve, so the demo sets expectations the pilot can meet. Ten best-looking examples would win the\nmeeting but create a gap that surfaces as distrust during the pilot. Label anything mocked, run with a\nrecorded fallback, and close on the pilot proposal."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are cherry-picked demos a bigger risk for LLM systems than for conventional software?","options":["LLM systems are always slower, so demos need shorter inputs","Output quality varies widely across inputs and runs, so a few good cases say little about typical behavior","Customers cannot see the source code of an LLM system","LLM providers forbid showing failure cases in public demos"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deterministic software that works once usually works every time. An LLM system's quality is a distribution over inputs, so curated examples can sit far above the median the pilot will experience."},{"q":"What is the main reason to show a failure or low-confidence case during a demo?","options":["It makes the demo shorter by skipping the full workflow","It proves the model is not overfitted to the demo inputs","It shows how errors are contained, which builds trust with buyers who know no system is flawless","It satisfies regulatory requirements for AI transparency in all jurisdictions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Experienced stakeholders expect errors; what they need to see is abstention, citations or human hand-off. One failure does not prove absence of overfitting, and no general rule requires it in demos."},{"q":"You show a recorded segment because the customer network is unreliable. What should you do?","options":["Present it as live to avoid undermining confidence","Skip the latency discussion entirely","Say it is recorded and give the real latency measured from the eval runs","Re-record it at a higher speed so it fits the time slot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recorded output is a legitimate fallback, but hiding it misrepresents latency and reliability. Stating it and giving measured numbers keeps the demo honest and the expectations accurate."}]},{"key":"scoping/rapid-prototyping-poc","sources":[],"why":"An AI project carries two kinds of uncertainty that specifications cannot remove. The first is\n*desirability*: will users actually want an assistant that drafts replies, or do they want a better\nsearch box? The second is *feasibility*: can a model achieve the accuracy, latency and cost the use case\nneeds on this customer's data? Neither can be settled by discussion. A written spec for \"an AI that\nsummarizes claims files\" hides whether the summaries are useful and whether the model gets the dates\nright on scanned forms.\n\nThe naive approach is to commit to the full build and discover the answers along the way. That is\nexpensive in the worst way: the integration, UI, auth and deployment work, most of the budget, gets done\nbefore anyone learns that the core model step reaches 70% when the business needs 95%. The opposite\nmistake is endless exploration in notebooks with no criteria, which produces impressive anecdotes and no\ndecision.\n\nPrototypes and PoCs sit in between. They are deliberately incomplete builds aimed at one uncertainty at a\ntime, so you spend days rather than months finding out whether the idea survives contact with users and\ndata. They are cheap because they skip everything that is not under test, and that same omission is why\nthey must not be mistaken for the product.","how":"### Prototype vs. PoC: different questions\nA **prototype** tests the experience: the workflow, the interaction, what output format is useful. It can\nbe crude, even partly faked (a \"Wizard of Oz\" prototype where a person produces the \"AI\" output behind the\nscenes), *because* the question is about the human, not the model. A **PoC** tests technical feasibility:\nit must use the real model, real data and a real metric, *because* faking any of those invalidates the\nanswer. Mixing the two questions into one build usually answers neither well. Neither is a pilot (real\nusers doing real work, measuring business impact) or an MVP (the smallest version you would actually\nship and support); a PoC that passes earns a pilot, not a launch.\n\n### 1. Find the riskiest assumption\nList what must be true for the project to succeed (the data is accessible, the model can read these\ndocuments, latency fits the workflow, cost per transaction fits the budget, users trust the output). Rank\nby likelihood of being false times cost if false. The PoC targets the top item; everything else can be\nstubbed.\n\n### 2. State a hypothesis with a threshold\n\"Using a general-purpose model with retrieval over the policy manual, at least 85% of answers to 100\nreal employee questions will be judged correct and grounded.\" The threshold comes from the business\nneed, *because* \"it works pretty well\" cannot be passed or failed.\n\n### 3. Build the eval set from real data first\nCollect real inputs, including messy ones, and label expected outputs with domain experts. Fifty to a few\nhundred examples is a common scale for a PoC, enough to see error categories, though small sets give\nwide confidence intervals. This comes first *because* every later decision is read off this set. Size it\nwith the binomial interval for a measured accuracy *p* on *n* examples:\n\n```\n95% half-width ≈ 1.96 × sqrt(p(1 − p) / n)\nn ≈ 1.96² × p(1 − p) / E²     (examples needed for half-width E)\n```\n\nAt *p* = 0.85, 100 examples give about ±7 points and 400 give about ±3.5; resolving ±3 points needs\nroughly 550. If the business threshold and the likely result are close, the set has to be large; if\nthey are far apart, 100 is plenty.\n\nSplit the set before tuning: a *dev* portion you read and iterate on, and a *held-out* portion nobody\nlooks at until the readout. Every prompt change chosen because it fixed dev failures fits the system to\nthose examples, so the dev score drifts upward faster than true accuracy; the held-out number is the one\nthe decision uses.\n\n### 4. Build the thinnest end-to-end slice\nWire input to output with the least code possible. Start with the strongest available model to establish\nthe ceiling, *because* if the best general-purpose model cannot do it with your prompt and retrieval,\ncheaper off-the-shelf models almost certainly will not; cost optimization comes after feasibility. The\nceiling is approach-specific, not absolute: fine-tuning, a specialized component (say, a dedicated OCR or\ntable parser) or a redesigned pipeline can beat it, so a failed PoC rules out the approach tested, not\nthe use case.\n\n### 5. Iterate with error analysis, inside a timebox\nRead failures on the dev split, group them, fix the largest group, re-run. Largest group first *because*\nfixes are roughly one category at a time, so the biggest category is the biggest available gain. Track\nlatency and cost per example alongside accuracy, since a design that passes on quality but takes 40\nseconds per answer has not passed. Stop when the threshold is met, the curve flattens, or the box closes.\n\n### 6. Write the findings and decide\nRecord the result, the error breakdown, what was stubbed, and what production would require. Decide:\nproceed to pilot, pivot the design, or stop. A disposable prototype gets thrown away; an evolutionary one\nis kept only if it was built with that intent.","example":"A minimal PoC harness: real labeled examples, one function under test, a pre-written threshold, and a\ndecision printed at the end. The model call is a placeholder for whichever provider SDK you use.\n\n```python\nimport json, math, collections, time\n\nTHRESHOLD = 0.85  # written down before any tuning, from the business requirement\n\ndef call_model(question: str, context: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Placeholder: call your LLM provider here with a fixed prompt version.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef retrieve(question: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Placeholder: the thinnest retrieval that could work (e.g. BM25 over the manual).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\ndef judge(pred: str, expected: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Start with expert labels or a strict string/regex check; add an LLM judge later if needed.\"\"\"\n    return expected.lower() in pred.lower()\n\ndef run_poc(path: str = \"eval_set.jsonl\", split: str = \"dev\") -> None:\n    rows = [json.loads(l) for l in open(path)]           # {\"q\", \"expected\", \"category\", \"split\"}\n    rows = [r for r in rows if r[\"split\"] == split]      # iterate on dev; read held-out once\n    errors, correct, secs = collections.Counter(), 0, []\n    for r in rows:\n        t0 = time.monotonic()\n        pred = call_model(r[\"q\"], retrieve(r[\"q\"]))\n        secs.append(time.monotonic() - t0)\n        if judge(pred, r[\"expected\"]):\n            correct += 1\n        else:\n            errors[r[\"category\"]] += 1\n    n = len(rows); acc = correct / n\n    half = 1.96 * math.sqrt(acc * (1 - acc) / n)          # binomial 95% half-width\n    p95 = sorted(secs)[int(0.95 * (n - 1))]\n    print(f\"[{split}] accuracy {acc:.1%} ±{half:.1%} on {n} real examples, p95 {p95:.1f}s\")\n    print(\"errors by category:\", errors.most_common())\n    if split == \"heldout\":\n        verdict = (\"pass\" if acc - half >= THRESHOLD else\n                   \"fail\" if acc + half < THRESHOLD else \"inconclusive: need more data\")\n        print(\"DECISION:\", verdict)\n```\n\nThe design choices are the point: the threshold is a constant set before the loop; errors are grouped by\ncategory so the next iteration targets the biggest one; latency is measured, not assumed; and the\ndecision is only printed for the held-out split, with an explicit \"inconclusive\" band when the interval\nstraddles the threshold. Run on a 200-example held-out split, 88% gives about ±4.5 points, so the verdict\nis \"inconclusive\" against 85%, which is the honest answer and tells you to label more data, not to\ncelebrate.","tradeoffs":["Disposable prototypes are fast because they ignore quality, but teams are tempted to ship them; keep the code only when it was built evolutionarily with tests and structure from the start.","Starting with the strongest model establishes feasibility quickly, but it can hide a cost problem; this flips when cost per transaction is itself the riskiest assumption and must be tested first.","Wizard-of-Oz prototypes answer desirability without any model work, but they say nothing about feasibility; use them only when the user experience is the main unknown.","Small eval sets let a PoC finish in days, but with 100 examples the sampling error on accuracy is several points, so a near-threshold result needs more data before a firm decision."],"pitfalls":["Running the PoC on clean sample data the vendor or customer prepared, not on production-like data; the symptom is accuracy that drops sharply in the pilot on scanned, partial or messy inputs.","Proving the easy part, such as showing a chat UI works, while the hard assumption (data access, a rare document type, latency) stays untested; the symptom is surprise blockers after the contract.","Defining success after seeing the results, so the PoC always \"succeeds\"; the symptom is a criterion that suspiciously matches the number achieved.","PoC purgatory: a successful PoC with no owner, budget or plan for the next stage; the symptom is a well-received demo followed by months of silence.","Shipping the prototype code into production because \"it already works\", which brings hard-coded credentials, no retries and no tests; the symptom is the first production incident.","Tuning and reporting on the same examples, so the reported accuracy is fitted to the eval set; the symptom is a readout number several points above what a fresh sample or the pilot shows."],"when":"Build a prototype when the main unknown is what users need or how they will interact; build a PoC when\nthe main unknown is whether the model can meet a quantified bar on real data. Do both before committing\nto an integration-heavy build, and inside a timebox. Skip them when the task is well understood and the\nsame pattern already runs in production elsewhere with similar data; a pilot may then be the cheaper first\nstep. Measure a PoC by the decision it enabled and the risks it retired, not by how polished it looks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval harness and thinnest slice, tracks error categories per iteration, and keeps prototype code clearly separated from anything meant to survive.","architect":"Picks which assumption the PoC must retire and judges whether a passing result actually transfers to the production architecture, data volumes and constraints.","fde":"Gets real customer data into the PoC early, negotiates the success threshold with the customer up front, and turns the findings into a pilot proposal."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a PoC of an LLM that answers questions over their 40,000-page engineering archive.\nTheir IT team offers a curated folder of 200 clean PDFs to make it fast. What do you do, and why?","a":"Accept the curated folder only for wiring the slice, not for the decision. The riskiest assumptions\nare probably document parsing (scans, drawings, tables) and retrieval over a large, near-duplicate corpus,\nwhich a clean 200-PDF set would hide. Ask for a random sample across the archive, including old scans, and\nbuild the eval questions with engineers who actually ask them. State the threshold up front. If full\naccess is impossible in the timebox, report results as conditional on the sample and list what remains\nuntested. Otherwise the PoC passes and the pilot fails on the very data the curated set excluded."},"quiz":[{"q":"What best distinguishes a PoC from a rapid prototype?","options":["A PoC is always longer and more expensive than a prototype","A PoC tests technical feasibility against criteria on real data; a prototype tests what the experience should be","A prototype must use the production model while a PoC can use a mock","A PoC is shown to executives while a prototype is internal only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The difference is the question asked. A prototype can fake the AI because it studies users; a PoC cannot fake the model or data because feasibility is exactly what it measures."},{"q":"Why start a PoC with the strongest available model rather than the cheapest?","options":["Cheap models are not allowed on customer data","The strongest model is always cheapest per correct answer","It establishes a ceiling for the approach: if the best model fails, cheaper off-the-shelf ones almost certainly will too","Strong models need no evaluation set"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Feasibility first: the strongest model bounds what this prompt-and-retrieval approach can achieve, and cost can be optimized later. The exception is when cost itself is the riskiest assumption, in which case it must be tested directly. Fine-tuning or specialized components can still exceed that ceiling."},{"q":"A PoC reaches 86% against an 85% threshold on 100 examples. What is the soundest conclusion?","options":["The PoC has clearly passed and the team should commit to full production","The PoC failed because results should exceed the threshold by at least ten points","It is a marginal pass; sampling error of several points means more data is needed before a firm commitment","The threshold was wrong and should be raised to 90%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With 100 examples, accuracy near 85% has a 95% interval of roughly ±7 points, so 86% is consistent with true performance below threshold. Collect more examples or proceed to a pilot with that risk stated."}]},{"key":"scoping/pilot-to-production-plan","sources":[],"why":"A PoC answers \"can it work?\" on a sample. It cannot tell you whether people will use it in their daily\nworkflow, whether it changes the business metric, or what breaks under real traffic, real permissions and\nreal edge cases. Those answers only come from real use, which is what a pilot is for.\n\nBut pilots have a well-known failure mode: they succeed and then nothing happens. The pilot ran on a\nchampion team with an engineer on call in a chat channel, on a sandbox environment exempted from security\nreview, paid for from an innovation budget. Moving to production needs SSO, a security sign-off, an\noperations owner, a support process, a cost line in someone's budget, and training for users who did not\nvolunteer. If none of that was planned, the pilot's success creates a demand nobody can meet, and the\nproject stalls in what people call pilot purgatory.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in opposite ways. Skipping the pilot and launching to everyone means the\nfirst real measurement happens at full blast radius, with no baseline comparison and no easy way back.\nRunning an open-ended pilot \"to learn\" means there is never a moment when the evidence is weighed and a\ndecision is made. A pilot → production plan exists to make the pilot a measured experiment with a\npre-agreed path to scale, so success triggers production work that is already scoped and funded.","how":"### 1. Define the stages and what each proves\nA common progression is PoC → pilot → limited rollout → general availability. Each stage widens exposure\nand answers a different question: feasibility; real-workflow impact; operability at scale; full adoption.\nName the question for each stage *because* a stage without a question has no natural end.\n\nAI systems usually need a second axis besides exposure: *autonomy*. A system can run in **shadow mode**\n(it produces outputs that are logged and scored but never shown or used), then **assist** (a human sees\nthe suggestion and decides), then **act with review** (it acts and a human samples or can reverse), and\nonly then **act alone** on the subset of cases where the evidence supports it. Each step up removes a\nhuman safety net, so each needs its own evidence. Shadow mode is the cheapest way to measure accuracy on\nlive traffic with zero user impact; it cannot measure adoption or workflow effect, because nobody is\nusing the output.\n\n### 2. Measure the baseline before the pilot starts\nCapture the current metric (handle time, error rate, cycle time) for the pilot cohort and a comparison\ngroup *before* switching anything on. Without a baseline, any post-pilot number is uninterpretable,\n*because* you cannot separate the system's effect from seasonality or the attention of being piloted.\nWith both groups measured before and during, the estimate is a difference-in-differences:\n\n```\neffect = (pilot_during − pilot_before) − (comparison_during − comparison_before)\n```\n\nThe second term removes whatever moved both groups at once (a quiet season, a policy change, a new\nticket mix), which a plain before/after comparison silently credits to the system.\n\n### 3. Choose the cohort deliberately\nPilot cohorts tend to be enthusiastic volunteers, which inflates results. Include at least some typical\nusers, and if possible keep a comparable group without the tool. Size the cohort so the metric can move\ndetectably within the pilot's duration. A common rule of thumb for comparing two group means at 95%\nconfidence and 80% power is\n\n```\nn per group ≈ 16 × σ² / δ²      (σ = per-unit standard deviation, δ = effect you must detect)\n```\n\nTwo corrections matter in practice. The unit is whatever you assign the tool to: if whole teams get it,\noutcomes within a team are correlated and the effective sample is much closer to the number of teams\nthan the number of tickets, so a two-team pilot can show a large effect that is partly just \"team A is\nfaster\". And early weeks carry a novelty effect, as users try the tool eagerly or slowly at first, so\nplan enough duration to see the weekly trend settle rather than reading the first fortnight.\n\n### 4. Write success, guardrail and exit criteria\n- **Success metrics:** the business outcome the system is meant to move.\n- **Guardrail metrics:** what must not get worse (customer satisfaction, error escapes, compliance\n  incidents, cost per transaction).\n- **Exit criteria:** the go/no-go rule, e.g. \"median handle time down ≥ 15% with no guardrail regression\n  over four weeks\".\n- **Stop triggers:** conditions that halt the pilot early, such as a data leak or a severe error.\n\nGuardrails matter *because* AI systems often improve the headline metric by shifting cost elsewhere, for\ninstance faster replies with more escalations.\n\n### 5. Build the production-gap list during the pilot, not after\nList everything production needs that the pilot skipped: identity integration, security and privacy\nreview, data residency, monitoring and alerting, on-call, runbooks, support process, cost budget and\nquotas, evaluation regression gates, user training. Each item gets an owner and an estimate. Running these\nin parallel with the pilot is what prevents purgatory, *because* most of them are slow organizational\nprocesses, not code.\n\n### 6. Name owners and the handoff\nDecide who owns the system after launch: which team runs it, who funds inference cost, who triages\nquality issues. If the builders are external (an FDE team, a vendor), the handoff plan is part of the\nproduction plan.\n\n### 7. Hold the go/no-go and roll out in steps\nAt the end of the pilot, compare against the exit criteria in a scheduled meeting with the decision\nmakers. If go, expand in increments with rollback triggers, followed by a hypercare period of heightened\nmonitoring and fast response.","example":"A support-ticket drafting assistant for an insurer's customer service team. All figures are illustrative.\n\n- **Baseline (weeks −2 to 0):** two pilot teams (30 agents) and two comparison teams; median handle time\n  11.0 minutes, quality-audit pass rate 92%, CSAT 4.2/5.\n- **Pilot (weeks 1–6):** pilot teams use AI drafts; agents edit and send. Weekly audit of 200 randomly\n  sampled AI-assisted tickets. At a pass rate near 92%, a 200-ticket sample estimates it to within about\n  ±4 points at 95% confidence, enough to catch a large regression but not a one-point drift.\n- **Exit criteria (agreed week 0):** handle time down ≥ 15% relative to the comparison teams; audit pass\n  rate not below 90%; CSAT not below 4.1; inference cost under the per-ticket budget agreed with finance.\n- **Stop trigger:** any instance of a draft exposing another customer's data.\n- **Production-gap list, run in parallel:** SSO and role mapping (IT, 3 weeks), privacy review of\n  prompt logging (legal, 4 weeks), monitoring dashboards and alerts (platform team), runbook and on-call\n  rota (support engineering), training for 400 agents (enablement), cost centre for inference (finance).\n- **Week 6 result:** handle time 9.1 minutes vs. 10.8 in the comparison teams; audit pass rate 91%; CSAT\n  4.2. The naive before/after reading is 11.0 → 9.1, a 17% cut. The difference-in-differences is\n  (9.1 − 11.0) − (10.8 − 11.0) = −1.7 minutes, about −15.5% of the baseline: the comparison teams also\n  got 0.2 minutes faster without the tool, and that part is not the system's doing. It clears the 15%\n  bar narrowly, and the weekly trend held steady across weeks 4–6, so it is not a fading novelty spike.\n  Go decision. Rollout to 25%, then 100% over six weeks, with rollback if the audit pass\n  rate pooled over two consecutive weeks (400 tickets) drops below 88%. The rollback line sits below the\n  90% exit bar on purpose: with a true rate of 91% and ±4-point weekly noise, a 90% trigger on a single\n  week's sample would fire spuriously roughly a third of the time, while 88% on 400 pooled tickets is a\n  false alarm only a few percent of the time yet still catches a real drop to the mid-80s. Hypercare for\n  the first four weeks after full rollout.\n\nBecause the gap list ran in parallel, production started two weeks after the decision instead of months.","tradeoffs":["A comparison group gives a credible causal estimate, but it delays benefits for those users; it flips when the expected effect is so large or obvious that a before/after comparison is convincing enough.","Volunteer cohorts make pilots easier to run and generate goodwill, but they overstate adoption and impact; include typical users when the decision depends on organization-wide adoption.","Longer pilots smooth out weekly noise and seasonality, but delay value and erode stakeholder patience; set duration from how long the metric needs to stabilize, not by habit.","Running production-readiness work in parallel shortens time to launch, but spends effort on a system that might get a no-go; this is worth it when the PoC evidence was strong."],"pitfalls":["No baseline measured before the pilot, so the result is a before/after story nobody can verify; the symptom is a debate at go/no-go about what the number \"would have been\".","A champion-only cohort whose results do not transfer; the symptom is strong pilot metrics followed by low adoption and complaints when typical users get access.","Pilot running in a sandbox exempt from security and identity requirements, with no plan to meet them; the symptom is a go decision followed by months waiting on reviews.","No guardrail metrics, so the headline metric improves while cost moves elsewhere; the symptom is faster handle times alongside rising escalations or complaints.","No named post-launch owner or budget, so the builders keep running it informally; the symptom is the pilot engineer still answering production questions six months later.","Reading the result in the first weeks while novelty is high; the symptom is a metric that improves sharply in week one and then decays week over week toward the baseline.","Jumping straight from assist to autonomous action because the pilot accuracy looked good; the symptom is errors reaching customers that a reviewer used to catch silently, visible as rising complaints with no change in measured model accuracy."],"when":"Write the plan when a PoC has passed and before the pilot starts, because baseline measurement and\ncriteria must be fixed before anyone sees results. Use the full staged approach when the system touches\nmany users, regulated data or customer-facing outcomes. For small internal tools with easy rollback,\ncompress it to a short pilot and a checklist. Measure the plan's quality by whether the go/no-go meeting\nis a short reading of agreed numbers and whether production follows within weeks. In interviews, a\nstrong answer names baseline, cohort, guardrails, gap list, owner and rollback.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the pilot so success and guardrail metrics are captured automatically, and turns production-gap items like monitoring and evaluation gates into tracked work.","architect":"Owns the stage gates and the production-gap list, deciding which pilot shortcuts are acceptable and which must be resolved before wider rollout.","fde":"Negotiates exit criteria and the comparison group with the customer, chases their security and identity reviews in parallel, and plans the handoff to the customer's own team."},"check":{"q":"A pilot of an AI coding assistant with 25 volunteer developers reports \"developers say they are\n40% more productive\". Leadership wants to roll it out to 2,000 developers next month. What do you ask\nbefore agreeing?","a":"First, what was measured: self-reported productivity from volunteers is weak evidence, inflated by\nenthusiasm and novelty. Ask for a baseline and comparison group on observable metrics such as cycle time,\nreview rework and defect rates, plus guardrails like security findings in generated code. Then the\nproduction gap: licensing or inference cost at 2,000 seats, identity and policy controls, data-handling\nreview for code sent to the provider, support and training for non-volunteers. Propose a staged rollout\nto a few hundred typical developers with agreed exit criteria and rollback, rather than a jump from 25 to 2,000."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the baseline be measured before the pilot begins?","options":["Because the system cannot log metrics once it is live","Because without it you cannot separate the system's effect from seasonality or the attention of being piloted","Because finance requires baselines for any budget approval","Because the model's accuracy changes after deployment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pilot's value is a comparison. Measured afterwards, the baseline is contaminated or reconstructed from memory, and the go/no-go turns into argument rather than evidence."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of guardrail metrics in a pilot plan?","options":["To replace the success metric when it is hard to measure","To monitor GPU utilization during the pilot","To catch cases where the headline metric improves by pushing cost or harm elsewhere","To decide which users join the pilot cohort"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AI systems can speed one step while increasing escalations, errors or cost downstream. Guardrails make those regressions part of the decision, so a \"win\" is not a cost shifted elsewhere."},{"q":"Which practice most directly prevents \"pilot purgatory\"?","options":["Extending the pilot until every user has tried the system","Choosing only enthusiastic volunteers for the cohort","Building and staffing the production-gap list in parallel with the pilot, with named owners","Postponing security review until after the go decision"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Purgatory happens when success arrives with no path, owner or budget for production. Working the gap list during the pilot means a go decision can be acted on quickly; postponing reviews causes the stall."}]},{"key":"scoping/productionization","sources":[],"why":"A prototype is built to answer a question quickly, so it makes every assumption that saves time: one user,\nclean inputs, a provider that always responds, a prompt edited by hand, no adversary, unlimited budget,\nthe builder watching the output. Each assumption is fine for a PoC and false in production.\n\nConsider what changes. Inputs arrive from thousands of users, so the long tail of strange inputs, which\nthe PoC sample barely touched, becomes a daily occurrence. Concurrency hits provider rate limits. The\nprovider has partial outages, returns malformed JSON, or slows down at peak. The model version behind an\nalias changes, or is deprecated on a schedule you do not control, and quality shifts silently. Users paste\ndocuments containing instructions that try to hijack the system. Someone asks why the system gave a\nparticular answer last Tuesday, and there is no record. The monthly inference bill arrives at several\ntimes the estimate because a retry loop or a few heavy users consumed it.\n\nThe naive approach is to \"just deploy the prototype\" and fix problems as they appear. That works for\ndeterministic code with good tests; for an LLM system it means problems appear as quality degradation that\nnobody detects, cost overruns, or a security incident, all discovered by users. The opposite approach,\nwaiting until everything is perfect, never ships. Productionization is the disciplined middle: enumerate\nthe failure sources, harden against the ones that matter most for this use case, and make the rest\nobservable.","how":"Organize the work by what can go wrong, *because* that makes gaps visible and lets you prioritize by risk.\n\n### 1. Reliability of the model call\nSet explicit timeouts, *because* a hung provider call otherwise holds a worker indefinitely. Retry only\ntransient errors (timeouts, rate limits, server errors) with exponential backoff and jitter, and cap total\nattempts, *because* unbounded retries multiply cost and load during an outage. Validate structured output\nagainst a schema and handle failure; a schema failure is a middle case, since outputs are sampled and one\nbounded re-try (or a repair prompt) often succeeds, but count it separately so a prompt regression is not\nhidden by retries. Define a fallback: a secondary model, a cached answer, or a graceful\n\"cannot answer, routing to a person\".\n\nTwo retry details decide whether this helps or hurts during an incident. First, retries multiply across\nlayers: if your code makes 3 attempts, the gateway in front of the provider makes 3, and the SDK\nunderneath also retries by default (many do), one user request can become up to 27 provider calls just\nwhen the provider is struggling. Pick one layer to own retries and turn the others off or down, and add\na circuit breaker so that when most recent calls are failing, new requests go straight to the fallback\ninstead of each discovering the outage separately. Second, retries must fit the caller's deadline:\nattempts × timeout + total backoff is the worst-case latency, and it should be below whatever the user\nor upstream service will wait. And only retry what is safe to repeat: re-sending a classification is\nharmless, but re-running a tool call that sends an email or files a refund duplicates it unless the\naction carries an idempotency key.\n\n### 2. Quality as a release gate\nTurn the PoC eval set into a regression suite that runs on every change to prompts, models, retrieval or\ntools. Version prompts and pin model versions where the provider allows, *because* an unpinned alias can\nchange behavior with no deployment on your side. Roll out changes through canary or shadow traffic.\n\n### 3. Observability\nTrace each request end to end: inputs, retrieved context, prompt version, model, outputs, latency, token\ncounts and cost, tied to a request ID. Without traces, debugging a bad answer means guessing. Monitor\nlatency percentiles, error rates, cost per request, and online quality signals such as user feedback or\nsampled human review.\n\n### 4. Security and data handling\nAuthenticate users and enforce authorization on retrieved data and tool calls with the user's own\npermissions, *because* the model will happily summarize documents the user should not see. Treat retrieved\nand user-supplied text as untrusted input that may contain prompt injection. Decide what gets logged, how\nPII is redacted, and how long data is kept.\n\n### 5. Cost and capacity\nSet per-user and per-tenant quotas, maximum input and output tokens, and budget alerts. Estimate cost at\nexpected volume, *because* PoC cost per call times production volume is often the first surprise:\n\n```\nmonthly cost ≈ requests/month × (tokens_in × price_in + tokens_out × price_out) × (1 + retry rate)\nin-flight requests ≈ arrival rate × mean latency          (Little's law)\ntokens/minute ≈ requests/minute × (tokens_in + tokens_out)\n```\n\nThe last two lines are what you check against provider rate limits, which are usually set per minute in\nboth requests and tokens. At an illustrative 20 requests/second with 6 seconds of latency, about 120\ncalls are in flight at once, and at 3,500 tokens each the system consumes over 4 million tokens per\nminute; a PoC that never ran more than one call at a time tells you nothing about whether that fits your\nquota tier. Long outputs dominate latency (they are generated token by token) and usually cost more per\ntoken than input, so capping output length is often the strongest cost and latency lever.\n\n### 6. Operations and ownership\nDefine SLOs, write runbooks for known failure modes (provider outage, quality regression, cost spike),\nset up on-call, and name the owning team. Plan for model deprecation, *because* the model you shipped on\nhas a finite lifetime.","example":"The same model call in prototype form and productionized form. The provider call is abstracted; the\nhardening around it is the point.\n\n```python\nimport json, logging, random, time, uuid\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"triage\")\nPROMPT_VERSION = \"triage-v7\"      # versioned, reviewed, covered by the regression suite\nMODEL = \"provider-model-2025-xx\"  # pinned snapshot, not a floating alias (placeholder name)\n\nclass Triage(BaseModel):\n    category: str\n    priority: int\n\nclass TransientError(Exception): ...   # map provider timeouts / 429 / 5xx onto this\n\ndef call_llm(model, prompt_version, text, timeout_s=None, with_usage=False):\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"wrap your provider SDK here\")\n\ndef prototype(ticket: str) -> dict:\n    return json.loads(call_llm(MODEL, PROMPT_VERSION, ticket))   # happy path only\n\ndef production(ticket: str, user_id: str, max_attempts: int = 3) -> Triage | None:\n    req_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    ticket = ticket[:20_000]                                     # bound input size and cost\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        t0 = time.monotonic()\n        try:\n            raw, usage = call_llm(MODEL, PROMPT_VERSION, ticket, timeout_s=15, with_usage=True)\n            result = Triage.model_validate_json(raw)             # schema check, not blind json.loads\n            log.info(\"ok\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"user\": user_id, \"prompt\": PROMPT_VERSION,\n                                  \"attempt\": attempt, \"ms\": int((time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000),\n                                  \"tokens_in\": usage[\"in\"], \"tokens_out\": usage[\"out\"]})\n            return result\n        except TransientError:\n            log.warning(\"transient\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"attempt\": attempt})\n            if attempt < max_attempts:                            # no pointless sleep before giving up\n                time.sleep(min(8, 2 ** attempt) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.0))  # backoff with jitter\n        except ValidationError:                                   # re-sample once more, counted separately\n            log.warning(\"bad_schema\", extra={\"req\": req_id, \"attempt\": attempt})\n    log.error(\"fallback_to_human\", extra={\"req\": req_id})\n    return None                                                   # caller routes to manual queue\n```\n\nEach added line answers a production question the prototype ignored: what if it hangs, rate-limits,\nreturns broken output, or costs too much, and how will we debug it next week? Note the worst case it\nimplies: three 15-second timeouts plus up to 2 and 4 seconds of backoff is about 51 seconds before the\nhuman fallback. That is acceptable for a background triage queue and far too long for a chat reply,\nwhere you would cut attempts, shorten the timeout, or pass a single overall deadline through the loop.\nWhat it deliberately omits, a circuit breaker and cost quotas, belongs in a shared gateway rather than in\nevery call site.","tradeoffs":["Retries improve success rates during transient faults, but they add latency and cost and can amplify an outage; this flips toward fewer retries when the call sits on a latency-critical user path.","Pinning model versions gives reproducible behavior, but it forces planned migrations before deprecation dates; floating aliases avoid that work at the price of unannounced behavior change.","Logging full prompts and outputs makes debugging and audits far easier, but it creates a store of sensitive data; redact or sample when the inputs contain regulated or personal data.","A fallback to a human or a simpler path keeps service available, but it needs staffing and a queue; for low-stakes features, returning a clear error may be the cheaper fallback.","Strict regression gates prevent quality backsliding, but a slow or flaky eval suite will be bypassed; keep the gating suite fast and run the expensive one nightly."],"pitfalls":["Deploying the prototype as-is because it worked in the pilot; the symptom is the first provider outage taking the feature down with hung requests and no fallback.","Retrying every error, including invalid requests and content refusals; the symptom is a cost spike and rate-limit errors during incidents, as retries pile onto an overloaded provider.","Using an unpinned model alias with no regression suite; the symptom is a quality drop users report weeks before anyone on the team notices.","Retrieving with a service account instead of the end user's permissions; the symptom is a user receiving content from documents they cannot open directly.","No per-request traces or cost attribution; the symptom is a large bill or a bad answer that nobody can explain or reproduce.","Stacked retries in application code, gateway and SDK; the symptom is provider call volume several times the request volume during an incident, with rate-limit errors that persist after the provider recovers.","Retrying side-effecting tool calls without idempotency keys; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or refunds that line up with timeout spikes in the traces."],"when":"Start productionization when a pilot shows the system is worth running, and scope it by risk: a\ncustomer-facing, regulated or tool-using system needs every section above; an internal summarizer with\neasy fallback may need only timeouts, quotas, basic tracing and a regression set. Begin with the items\nwhose failure is silent (quality regression, data exposure, cost), *because* loud failures get fixed\nanyway. Measure readiness with a checklist reviewed at a deployment readiness review, and track error\nrate, latency percentiles, cost per request and sampled quality after launch.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds timeouts, scoped retries, schema validation, fallbacks, tracing and cost logging around every model call, and wires the eval set into CI as a regression gate.","architect":"Decides the platform pieces shared across systems, such as gateways, prompt registry, tracing and quotas, so each team does not productionize the same concerns separately.","fde":"Maps production requirements onto the customer's environment: their identity provider, logging and retention rules, network constraints and the team that will own operations."},"check":{"q":"A pilot of a document Q&A assistant was a success. The prototype calls a model alias directly,\nretrieves with an admin service account, logs nothing, and has no tests. You have four weeks before\ncompany-wide launch. What do you do first, and why that order?","a":"Order by severity and silence of failure. First, fix authorization: retrieval must run with the\nend user's permissions, because a data-exposure incident is the most damaging and hardest to undo. Second,\nturn the pilot's eval examples into a regression suite and pin the model version, so quality changes\nbecome visible. Third, add request tracing, with redaction, plus cost logging. Fourth, timeouts, scoped\nretries, schema validation and a fallback. Then quotas, runbooks, on-call and an owner. Roll out by canary\nso remaining gaps surface on a small population first."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should retries be limited to transient errors such as timeouts and rate limits?","options":["Because providers charge double for retried requests","Because retrying permanent errors cannot succeed and only adds cost, latency and load during incidents","Because retries break schema validation","Because transient errors are always caused by the client"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A malformed request, an auth error or an oversized input will fail the same way again, and a policy refusal usually does. Retrying it wastes tokens and, during an outage, piles extra load onto an already struggling provider."},{"q":"What is the main risk of calling a floating model alias in production without a regression suite?","options":["The alias will stop resolving after a fixed number of requests","Latency always doubles when the alias updates","The underlying model can change without a deploy on your side, shifting quality with nothing to detect it","Aliases cannot return structured output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aliases may point to new snapshots over time. Without pinned versions or a regression suite, behavior changes silently, and the first signal is user complaints."},{"q":"Which productionization item should usually come first for a system that retrieves internal documents?","options":["Adding a caching layer to cut latency","Switching to a cheaper model to reduce cost","Enforcing the end user's permissions on retrieval","Building a dashboard of daily active users"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Data exposure through retrieval is severe, often silent and hard to reverse. Latency, cost and usage analytics matter, but they do not leak data to the wrong people."}]}]});
